     1	# One unsupported revision relation: what it changed for the judge
     2	
     3	2026-11-10. The test the council of 2026-11-09 recommended, carried out as written in [PROTOCOL.md](PROTOCOL.md). Saved D392 clean draw 1,
     4	the frozen judge through the experimental adapter. No change to Jev, a prompt, a rule, the frozen judge or a saved run. Cost: $0.0126.
     5	
     6	**Result: removing the relation changes what the judge reads on one question, and not what it concludes there, in these samples.**
     7	
     8	## The case in one picture
     9	
    10	An NVIDIA spokesperson answers a report that the Kyber rack is delayed: "Our roadmap is intact." Step 1 filed that under the Kyber
    11	shipping question. Jev read it as a later version of another statement (NVIDIA plans to sell more racks of the existing form), which
    12	is filed under the FY2027 revenue question. Because the adapter gives a record every question link of the record it revises, the
    13	Kyber answer was also shown under the revenue question, on 2026-07-06 and on every later day.
    14	
    15	## What was found
    16	
    17	| Step | Finding |
    18	|---|---|
    19	| Replay the saved run untouched, no send | Reproduced: 239 of 239 judge requests equal a saved one and took its saved answer; the ledger, the recorded requests and the counts equal the saved run's. |
    20	| Remove the one relation, no send | Five days identical. On 2026-07-06 the revenue question's request loses the Kyber answer (4 lines; 28 items for 29); the 14 other requests of that level are identical. The replay stops there. |
    21	| Remove the relation, keep the link, no send | All 14 days identical. For this relation the judge's input changes only through the carried link. |
    22	| Send that one request both ways, 3 times each | Six accepted answers, **all on rung 2** ($320 to below $400 billion): by the rule fixed beforehand, no difference in the conclusion in these samples. |
    23	
    24	The six answers, read one by one (`poc/data/clean-step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/judge-probe-1/`):
    25	
    26	| Arm | Implied revenue | What moved the rung from 3 to 2, in the answer's words | The Kyber answer |
    27	|---|---|---|---|
    28	| With it, 1 | about $335 billion | the Data Center question's forecast of about $390 billion, times a share of about 86% | cited, marked as pushing up, not used in the reasons |
    29	| With it, 2 | about $330 billion | the same, about 85% | cited, marked as pushing up, not used in the reasons |
    30	| With it, 3 | about $340 billion | the same, about 87% | cited, marked as pushing up, not used in the reasons |
    31	| Without it, 1 | about $340 billion | the same forecast, "most, but not all" | absent |
    32	| Without it, 2 | about $350 to $370 billion | the same forecast, discounted | absent |
    33	| Without it, 3 | about $335 billion | the same, about 86% | absent |
    34	
    35	The saved run moved this question from rung 3 to rung 2 on that day. Every one of the six answers gives the same cause: the
    36	question it depends on (Data Center revenue) had moved to about $390 billion. None names the roadmap statement.
    37	
    38	What the record does leave behind when it is there: in all three answers it appears among the cited evidence with the mark "pushes
    39	toward the top rung". A reader of the revenue question would see a statement about a later product generation listed as support.
    40	
    41	## How big the class is (counted on the saved ledgers, no cost)
    42	
    43	A revision relation reaches the judge in two ways.
    44	
    45	1. **It carries the new record into the old record's questions.** Links that exist only for that reason:
    46	
    47	   | Draw | Links in the ledger | Carried only by a relation | Share of all evidence shown to the judge |
    48	   |---|---|---|---|
    49	   | 1 | 700 | 5 | 42 of 4,575 showings |
