clean-arch/PRD.md:741:### Closing check
clean-arch/PRD.md:746:Before you return the answer, check it once. Read each evidence item back as the analyst following the company and keep it when you would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, about the company or its customers, suppliers, competitors, regulators or the economy. Ask first whether it is new: repeated common knowledge, a long-standing situation retold and an unargued view are noise. Do not drop a reported step, figure, guidance, forecast or decision because the item retells it or another outlet reported it before: a later step compares each statement with the evidence already stored and records a repeat there. Guidance, a forecast, a plan or a decision still in force keeps its place even when retold, including a change to a plan, design or schedule that another outlet reported days or weeks before. Judge the action or information itself, not its verb tense, and do not take a statement as old because its date is missing. Fitting one of the four kinds does not keep an item. A missing amount, date or scale never drops an item on its own: a forecast, a rumour, a counter-example or a distant or conditional statement is judged like any other.
clean-arch/PRD.md:759:Then check the theses and links. A new thesis that asks the same underlying question, about the same subject and scope, as an open thesis or another new thesis is not proposed: link its evidence to that thesis. A new thesis needs at least one kept evidence item, and a vague or noisy topic with no outcome to watch is not proposed. Each kept evidence item links to every thesis it informs and to no thesis its own relevance says it does not inform.
clean-arch/PRD.md:760:For each new thesis, check its opens_as, settled_by, closest_open_thesis and why_a_separate_bet: drop the thesis if it fits none of the four reasons in section 3, if no public material will settle it on a date, if an open thesis exists and its answer cannot move apart from it, or if it falls under a 'do not open' item in section 3; then link its evidence to every open thesis it informs, or leave it without links.
clean-arch/PRD.md:761:An item whose only home would be a new thesis that is not a big matter is returned with empty links and kept without links; an item an open thesis covers is linked there, not left without links. Then read back each statement you return with empty links that states the company's own figures: return it only if a figure can change what an analyst expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position; for example, a cash-rich company's balance or cash flow that the item ties to nothing usually cannot; return only what passes.
