"original_party": "Nvidia", "reporting_speaker": "Nvidia spokesperson, via EBC (ebc.com)", "tier": "party_statement", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v18", "v7"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}], "links": {"v18": "45:e2", "v7": "75:e6"}, "revises": [{"group": "45:e1", "by": "54:e3"}, {"group": "45:e3", "by": "54:e3"}, {"group": "58:e3", "by": "70:e4"}, {"group": "69:e4", "by": "70:e4"}, {"group": "63:E2", "by": "70:e4"}, {"group": "58:e4", "by": "70:e4"}, {"group": "75:e5", "by": "132:e8"}]}, "45:e3": {"original": {"id": "45:e3", "article": 45, "input_id": "gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587", "title": "Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says", "publisher": "cnbc.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together to reach similar compute power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and operationally burdensome, leaving Nvidia, in the firm's words, with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.\"", "passages": ["A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.", "\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\" SemiAnalysis said.", "That leaves Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market."], "verified_passages": ["A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.", "\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\" SemiAnalysis said.", "That leaves Nvidia with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis, reported by CNBC", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": "Monday", "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v18"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [{"id": "54:e2", "article": 54, "input_id": "gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e", "title": "Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims", "publisher": "finance.yahoo.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis also claimed that Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack configuration after pushback from hyperscaler customers.", "passages": ["The firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers."], "verified_passages": ["The firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "finance.yahoo.com (Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com)", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v20"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, {"id": "58:e2", "article": 58, "input_id": "gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d", "title": "Nvidia pushes Kyber release to 2028 following manufacturing concerns – report", "publisher": "datacenterdynamics.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's alternative plan to place two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve similar power output has been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its 'odd design and heavy operational burden.'", "passages": ["adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”"], "verified_passages": ["adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”"], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "datacenterdynamics.com (Charlotte Trueman)", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v18"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, {"id": "69:e2", "article": 69, "input_id": "gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96", "title": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop", "publisher": "the-decoder.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's planned alternative server design, NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely, after cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.", "passages": ["A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead."], "verified_passages": ["A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "the-decoder.com, citing SemiAnalysis", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Rubin Ultra generation (planned NVL72x2 configuration)", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v18"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, {"id": "70:e2", "article": 70, "input_id": "gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b", "title": "Nvidia underperforms peers as report sparks rack delay worries", "publisher": "uk.finance.yahoo.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis further alleged, in the weekend report cited by Investing.com, that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients.", "passages": ["The research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients."], "verified_passages": ["The research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "Investing.com", "tier": "rumour", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": "weekend report", "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v18"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, {"id": "72:e3", "article": 72, "input_id": "gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250", "title": "Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening", "publisher": "tikr.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "Nvidia's backup plan for scaling Rubin Ultra — bolting two current-generation racks together to reach similar compute power — was scrapped after cloud customers reportedly rejected it as awkward to operate and expensive to run, according to SemiAnalysis, leaving Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now and opening a possible opportunity for rivals such as AMD and Google at the high end of the AI chip market.", "passages": ["Nvidia had a backup plan: bolting two current-generation racks together to achieve similar power, but that idea was scrapped too.", "Cloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run.", "SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market."], "verified_passages": ["Nvidia had a backup plan: bolting two current-generation racks together to achieve similar power, but that idea was scrapped too.", "Cloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run.", "SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "tikr.com", "tier": "rumour", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Rubin Ultra scale-up path, 2027-2028", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v18"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, {"id": "75:e2", "article": 75, "input_id": "gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571", "title": "Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]", "publisher": "tomshardware.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "Per SemiAnalysis, Nvidia killed NVL72x2 — a stopgap rack that would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink — after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit, leaving, according to the report, no proven alternative to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.", "passages": ["Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.", "NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit."], "verified_passages": ["Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.", "NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "Luke James, Tom's Hardware", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "2027", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v18"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}], "links": {"v18": "45:e3", "v20": "54:e2"}, "revises": []}, "45:e5": {"original": {"id": "45:e5", "article": 45, "input_id": "gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587", "title": "Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says", "publisher": "cnbc.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.", "passages": ["SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027."], "verified_passages": ["SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis, reported by CNBC", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": "Monday", "evidence_period": "Second half of fiscal 2027", "figures": [{"measure": "Data Center compute revenue versus Wall Street consensus", "period": "Second half of fiscal 2027", "value": "20% above Wall Street consensus", "kind": "estimate", "basis": "SemiAnalysis projection; comparison to Wall Street consensus, scope limited to data-center compute revenue"}], "thesis_ids": ["v1", "v2"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [{"id": "72:e5", "article": 72, "input_id": "gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250", "title": "Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening", "publisher": "tikr.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "Research firm SemiAnalysis expects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street's current expectations.", "passages": ["SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations."], "verified_passages": ["SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "tikr.com", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Second half of fiscal 2027", "figures": [{"measure": "Data-center compute revenue versus Wall Street consensus", "period": "Second half of fiscal 2027", "value": "20% above", "kind": "estimate", "basis": "SemiAnalysis estimate relative to current Wall Street expectations; data-center compute segment"}], "thesis_ids": ["v1", "v2"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}], "links": {"v1": "45:e5", "v2": "45:e5"}, "revises": []}, "47:e1": {"original": {"id": "47:e1", "article": 47, "input_id": "gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2", "title": "NVIDIA’s RTX 3060 12 GB Graphics Card Comeback Proves Just How Bad Things Are For The PC Gaming Market", "publisher": "wccftech.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "NVIDIA's five-year-old GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB graphics card has been relaunched and returned to retail shelves, with US and European retailers restocking it at starting prices of $329 and €333 respectively — the same price at which the card initially launched in 2021. wccftech frames the relaunch as a consequence of the global DRAM supply crisis and NVIDIA's reduced ability to focus on the consumer segment amid heightened demand for AI chips.", "passages": ["NVIDIA's GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB has now landed back on retail shelves and comes at a time when global DRAM supply is facing a major crisis .", "So as of right now, both US and European retailers are restocking the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with prices starting at $329 and €333, respectively. This is the same price at which the RTX 3060 12 GB initially launched back in 2021."], "verified_passages": ["NVIDIA's GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB has now landed back on retail shelves and comes at a time when global DRAM supply is facing a major crisis .", "So as of right now, both US and European retailers are restocking the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with prices starting at $329 and €333, respectively. This is the same price at which the RTX 3060 12 GB initially launched back in 2021."], "original_party": "NVIDIA", "reporting_speaker": "wccftech.com", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "2026", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "47:e2": {"original": {"id": "47:e2", "article": 47, "input_id": "gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2", "title": "NVIDIA’s RTX 3060 12 GB Graphics Card Comeback Proves Just How Bad Things Are For The PC Gaming Market", "publisher": "wccftech.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "wccftech reports that the DRAM crisis is intensifying in 2026 and that graphics-card prices continue to rise across all commodity memory, including the GDDR7 used in NVIDIA's RTX 50 GPUs. The outlet states that DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements and are committing billions of dollars to new fabrication facilities aimed mainly at meeting AI-driven demand, and it believes the shortage will not end soon and is unlikely to relieve the consumer market.", "passages": ["As the DRAM crisis intensifies in 2026, graphics card prices continue to go up. We see some weird prioritization of specific models with higher capacity, where the increased margins lie. All new RTX 50 GPUs come with GDDR7 memory, which is the best you can get, and the problem is that all kinds of commodity memory are expensive these days.", "And since DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements , we believe the crisis won't end soon, despite all DRAM manufacturers making promises to pour billions of dollars towards new manufacturing facilities , which are only being built to meet the demand created by AI, and would likely not push the consumer market out of these dire times."], "verified_passages": ["As the DRAM crisis intensifies in 2026, graphics card prices continue to go up. We see some weird prioritization of specific models with higher capacity, where the increased margins lie. All new RTX 50 GPUs come with GDDR7 memory, which is the best you can get, and the problem is that all kinds of commodity memory are expensive these days.", "And since DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements , we believe the crisis won't end soon, despite all DRAM manufacturers making promises to pour billions of dollars towards new manufacturing facilities , which are only being built to meet the demand created by AI, and would likely not push the consumer market out of these dire times."], "original_party": "wccftech.com", "reporting_speaker": "wccftech.com", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "2026 and coming years", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v5"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v5": "47:e2"}, "revises": []}, "49:e2": {"original": {"id": "49:e2", "article": 49, "input_id": "gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348", "title": "AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "According to the article, Nvidia is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall (2026) as part of its Vera Rubin platform.", "passages": ["In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform."], "verified_passages": ["In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform."], "original_party": "Nvidia", "reporting_speaker": "Fool.com (Adria Cimino)", "tier": "party_statement", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Fall 2026", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v19", "v7"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v19": "49:e2", "v7": "49:e2"}, "revises": []}, "49:e3": {"original": {"id": "49:e3", "article": 49, "input_id": "gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348", "title": "AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "According to the article, Nvidia is targeting the personal computing market with a superchip -- including a GPU and a CPU -- also set for fall 2026 release.", "passages": ["And at the same time, Nvidia is targeting the personal computing market with a superchip -- also set for fall release -- that includes a GPU and a CPU."], "verified_passages": ["And at the same time, Nvidia is targeting the personal computing market with a superchip -- also set for fall release -- that includes a GPU and a CPU."], "original_party": "Nvidia", "reporting_speaker": "Fool.com (Adria Cimino)", "tier": "party_statement", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Fall 2026", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "50:e1": {"original": {"id": "50:e1", "article": 50, "input_id": "gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145", "title": "Sentiment Could Be Turning Sour on Nvidia. Here’s Where 1 Analyst Thinks NVDA Stock Is Headed Next.", "publisher": "barchart.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "Seaport Research published a note (described by Barchart as coming 'last week', i.e. before the 2026-07-05 article) arguing that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues and flagging a 'circular-financing boom' — that Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips, either directly via equity or debt or by allowing financing of the increasingly expensive chips — which bears contend could mean the demand investors are seeing is not real and could raise earnings-quality concerns down the line. The Barchart author says the concern may have merit.", "passages": ["Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors.", "That said, concerns around a \"circular-financing boom\" put forward by Seaport analysts in their note are one of the issues bears have contended could lead to issues around earnings quality down the line. The thinking is that the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips (either directly via equity or debt, or simply allowing for financing of these increasingly expensive chips), that could mean that the demand investors are seeing isn't real. At least, not to the extent that Nvidia's earnings releases suggest."], "verified_passages": ["Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors.", "That said, concerns around a \"circular-financing boom\" put forward by Seaport analysts in their note are one of the issues bears have contended could lead to issues around earnings quality down the line. The thinking is that the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips (either directly via equity or debt, or simply allowing for financing of these increasingly expensive chips), that could mean that the demand investors are seeing isn't real. At least, not to the extent that Nvidia's earnings releases suggest."], "original_party": "Seaport Research", "reporting_speaker": "Barchart.com (article author)", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": "last week", "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v9"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v9": "50:e1"}, "revises": []}, "51:e1": {"original": {"id": "51:e1", "article": 51, "input_id": "gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae", "title": "Singapore files new charges in Nvidia chip fraud case", "publisher": "sg.finance.yahoo.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "Singaporean prosecutors filed additional charges, including money laundering, against Alan Wei Zhaolun, a key suspect in an AI server fraud case tied to Nvidia AI processors being diverted illicitly to locations including China. Wei pleaded not guilty in a local court on Monday, with his lawyer calling the charges \"misconceived\"; his bail was raised by $450,000 to more than $1.2 million.", "passages": ["Singaporean prosecutors filed additional charges including money laundering against a key suspect in an artificial intelligence (AI) server fraud case, part of the Southeast Asian nation's growing efforts to clamp down on Nvidia Corp chips being diverted illicitly to other locations including China.", "Alan Wei Zhaolun pleaded not guilty in a local court on Monday, with his lawyer saying the charges were \"misconceived\". The China-born businessman has been released on bail, his lawyers said, after the amount was raised by $450,000 to more than $1.2 million earlier in the day."], "verified_passages": ["Singaporean prosecutors filed additional charges including money laundering against a key suspect in an artificial intelligence (AI) server fraud case, part of the Southeast Asian nation's growing efforts to clamp down on Nvidia Corp chips being diverted illicitly to other locations including China.", "Alan Wei Zhaolun pleaded not guilty in a local court on Monday, with his lawyer saying the charges were \"misconceived\". The China-born businessman has been released on bail, his lawyers said, after the amount was raised by $450,000 to more than $1.2 million earlier in the day."], "original_party": "Singapore prosecutors", "reporting_speaker": "sg.finance.yahoo.com", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": "Monday", "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "51:e3": {"original": {"id": "51:e3", "article": 51, "input_id": "gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae", "title": "Singapore files new charges in Nvidia chip fraud case", "publisher": "sg.finance.yahoo.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-05", "summary": "US authorities suspect a company behind Thailand's national AI efforts of helping smuggle billions of dollars worth of Super Micro servers containing advanced Nvidia chips to several customers in China, including Alibaba Group Holding. The article frames Southeast Asia as a critical transit hub for routing AI chips and servers to China as Washington curbs Chinese entities' access to cutting-edge semiconductor technology.", "passages": ["Southeast Asia has emerged as a critical transit hub for routing AI chips and servers to China as Washington has curbed Chinese entities' access to cutting-edge semiconductor technologies.", "Apart from the fraud probes in Singapore, US authorities also suspect a company behind Thailand's national AI efforts of helping to smuggle billions of dollars worth of Super Micro servers containing advanced Nvidia chips to several customers in China, including Alibaba Group Holding Ltd."], "verified_passages": ["Southeast Asia has emerged as a critical transit hub for routing AI chips and servers to China as Washington has curbed Chinese entities' access to cutting-edge semiconductor technologies.", "Apart from the fraud probes in Singapore, US authorities also suspect a company behind Thailand's national AI efforts of helping to smuggle billions of dollars worth of Super Micro servers containing advanced Nvidia chips to several customers in China, including Alibaba Group Holding Ltd."], "original_party": "US authorities", "reporting_speaker": "sg.finance.yahoo.com", "tier": "rumour", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "55:e1": {"original": {"id": "55:e1", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis said that even three years into the AI build-out, the general market's shipment-volume and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the second half of calendar 2026 remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.", "passages": ["Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model ."], "verified_passages": ["Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model ."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Second half of calendar 2026", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v1"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v1": "55:e1"}, "revises": []}, "55:e2": {"original": {"id": "55:e2", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has itself started backstopping GPU rental offtakes: Nvidia gives neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment, a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity, and in exchange shares in a portion of the neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; the neocloud remains free to rent to any other customer on any terms and the stated intent is that the backstop is never invoked.", "passages": ["This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.", "The Neocloud is of course free to rent to any other customer they would like for any term length they deem commercially reasonable and indeed the intent is for the Neocloud to never actually have to invoke the backstop."], "verified_passages": ["This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.", "The Neocloud is of course free to rent to any other customer they would like for any term length they deem commercially reasonable and indeed the intent is for the Neocloud to never actually have to invoke the backstop."], "original_party": "Nvidia", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "From 2026 onward", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v9"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v9": "55:e2"}, "revises": [{"group": "8:e2", "by": "55:e2"}]}, "55:e3": {"original": {"id": "55:e3", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has started backstopping datacenter leases, extending its backstop program beyond GPU rental offtakes to the datacenter leg of neocloud projects.", "passages": ["Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases."], "verified_passages": ["Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases."], "original_party": "Nvidia", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "From 2026 onward", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v9"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v9": "55:e3"}, "revises": [{"group": "8:e7", "by": "55:e3"}]}, "55:e7": {"original": {"id": "55:e7", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis said many other neoclouds have Nvidia backstops that are not public yet, that many neoclouds are already in negotiations with Nvidia while others are considering joining, and that it expects backstop announcements to come faster every quarter.", "passages": ["We have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.", "Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia, and there are still others that are considering engaging with the backstop program."], "verified_passages": ["We have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.", "Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia, and there are still others that are considering engaging with the backstop program."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "rumour", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "As of mid-2026", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v9"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v9": "55:e7"}, "revises": []}, "55:e8": {"original": {"id": "55:e8", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis forecast that annual AI capex, including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute, will be well north of $2 trillion in 2028, with cumulative AI capex of about $11.1 trillion from 2024 to 2029, and that credit markets will be the main funding source for the buildout.", "passages": ["Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout."], "verified_passages": ["Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "2028 annual; cumulative 2024-2029", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"clean-2": "55:e8"}, "revises": []}, "55:e9": {"original": {"id": "55:e9", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis forecast that AI debt financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market with more than $7 trillion of debt outstanding by 2029, driven by AI IT and AI datacenter capex needs of neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13 trillion US mortgage-backed financing market.", "passages": ["AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T."], "verified_passages": ["AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T."], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "By 2029", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "55:e10": {"original": {"id": "55:e10", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis noted the gap between CoreWeave's roughly 10% yield on 5-year unsecured bonds and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5 billion DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, a 5.9% yield about 90 basis points wider than Meta's roughly 5.0% 5-year bond yield, read as the market pricing CoreWeave's execution risk on that project at 90bps.", "passages": ["Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%."], "verified_passages": ["Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%."], "original_party": "CoreWeave and Meta", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "5-year loan/bond tenors; as reported in July 2026", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v9"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v9": "55:e10"}, "revises": []}, "55:e11": {"original": {"id": "55:e11", "article": 55, "input_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6", "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters", "publisher": "newsletter.semianalysis.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "SemiAnalysis said short-dated GPU rentals remain a seller's market: only a few neoclouds are still offering one-year rentals and they set aggressive terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, enough in some cases to fund the cluster's entire capex.", "passages": ["When it comes to shorter-dated rentals for everyone else, it is still a seller’s market. At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!"], "verified_passages": ["When it comes to shorter-dated rentals for everyone else, it is still a seller’s market. At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!"], "original_party": "SemiAnalysis", "reporting_speaker": "SemiAnalysis", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "2026", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "56:e1": {"original": {"id": "56:e1", "article": 56, "input_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd", "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why", "publisher": "marketscale.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.", "passages": ["Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation."], "verified_passages": ["Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation."], "original_party": "Morgan Stanley Research", "reporting_speaker": "MarketScale", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Vera Rubin (VR200) generation, H2 2026 and beyond", "figures": [{"measure": "VR200 NVL72 rack cost per unit", "period": null, "value": "$7.8 million", "kind": "estimate", "basis": "hyperscale cloud providers, per rack, Morgan Stanley Research estimate"}, {"measure": "GB300 NVL72 rack cost per unit", "period": null, "value": "$4 million", "kind": "estimate", "basis": "current generation, per rack, Morgan Stanley Research estimate"}], "thesis_ids": ["v1", "v2", "v20"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v1": "56:e1", "v2": "56:e1", "v20": "56:e1"}, "revises": []}, "56:e2": {"original": {"id": "56:e2", "article": 56, "input_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd", "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why", "publisher": "marketscale.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.", "passages": ["The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume."], "verified_passages": ["The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume."], "original_party": "Morgan Stanley Research (the bank)", "reporting_speaker": "MarketScale", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Vera Rubin (VR200) generation, volume sales", "figures": [{"measure": "Rubin GPU price", "period": null, "value": "$55,000", "kind": "estimate", "basis": "per GPU, when sold in volume, Morgan Stanley estimate"}, {"measure": "Vera CPU price", "period": null, "value": "$5,000", "kind": "estimate", "basis": "per CPU, when sold in volume, Morgan Stanley estimate"}], "thesis_ids": ["v19", "v20"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v19": "56:e2", "v20": "56:e2"}, "revises": []}, "56:e3": {"original": {"id": "56:e3", "article": 56, "input_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd", "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why", "publisher": "marketscale.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-06", "summary": "Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200 while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.", "passages": ["The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.", "As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent."], "verified_passages": ["The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.", "As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent."], "original_party": "Morgan Stanley Research", "reporting_speaker": "MarketScale", "tier": "third_party_view", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "VR200 rack generation", "figures": [{"measure": "memory content per rack", "period": null, "value": "$2 million", "ki