ing Face", "reporting_speaker": "The Robot Report Staff", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, {"id": "118:e1", "article": 118, "input_id": "gnews:21e9182d07508a6690f10419", "title": "Nvidia, Hugging Face Collaborate on Open Source Robot Models", "publisher": "aibusiness.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-08", "summary": "NVIDIA and Hugging Face announced on July 6 a new collaboration under which NVIDIA's Isaac GR00T 1.7 open reasoning vision-language-action (VLA) models for humanoid robotics and its Isaac Teleop framework for robotics data collection were integrated into Hugging Face's open-source LeRobot physical-AI library, with NVIDIA's Cosmos 3 frontier robotics model planned for future integration.", "passages": ["Nvidia and Hugging Face entered into a new collaboration for open source robotics, integrating the chipmaker’s models onto LeRobot, Hugging Face’s open source library for physical AI developers.", "Under the deal, unveiled on July 6, the companies added Nvidia's Isaac GR00T 1.7 open reasoning vision-language-action (VLA) models for humanoid robotics and the Isaac Teleop framework for robotics data collection into LeRobot, with the Cosmos 3 frontier robotics model planned for future integration."], "verified_passages": ["Nvidia and Hugging Face entered into a new collaboration for open source robotics, integrating the chipmaker’s models onto LeRobot, Hugging Face’s open source library for physical AI developers.", "Under the deal, unveiled on July 6, the companies added Nvidia's Isaac GR00T 1.7 open reasoning vision-language-action (VLA) models for humanoid robotics and the Isaac Teleop framework for robotics data collection into LeRobot, with the Cosmos 3 frontier robotics model planned for future integration."], "original_party": "NVIDIA and Hugging Face", "reporting_speaker": "aibusiness.com", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": "2026-07-06", "statement_date_text": "July 6", "evidence_period": "from July 6, 2026 (integration under way; Cosmos 3 planned for future integration)", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "86:e1": {"original": {"id": "86:e1", "article": 86, "input_id": "gnews:784c556d2e58ed09dd8aa7fc", "title": "Chinese lidar maker with Nvidia ties accused of being cyber risk for U.S.", "publisher": "cnbc.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "In its latest annual filing, Nvidia reported that its automotive segment revenue for fiscal year 2026 was up 39% from a year prior, driven by adoption of its self-driving platforms.", "passages": ["In its latest annual filing, Nvidia reported automotive revenue for fiscal year 2026 was up 39% from a year prior, driven by the adoption of its self-driving platforms."], "verified_passages": ["In its latest annual filing, Nvidia reported automotive revenue for fiscal year 2026 was up 39% from a year prior, driven by the adoption of its self-driving platforms."], "original_party": "Nvidia", "reporting_speaker": "CNBC", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "FY2026", "figures": [{"measure": "automotive revenue growth (year over year)", "period": "FY2026", "value": "up 39%", "kind": "actual", "basis": "Nvidia automotive segment, fiscal year 2026 versus prior year; from latest annual filing"}], "thesis_ids": ["v1"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v1": "86:e1"}, "revises": []}, "86:e2": {"original": {"id": "86:e2", "article": 86, "input_id": "gnews:784c556d2e58ed09dd8aa7fc", "title": "Chinese lidar maker with Nvidia ties accused of being cyber risk for U.S.", "publisher": "cnbc.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "Hesai Technology, whose lidar sensors are one option automakers can integrate into Nvidia's autonomous-vehicle platforms, told CNBC it has reduced the cost of its lidar units from more than $10,000 each to less than $200.", "passages": ["Hesai told CNBC it has reduced the cost of its lidar units from more than $10,000 each to less than $200."], "verified_passages": ["Hesai told CNBC it has reduced the cost of its lidar units from more than $10,000 each to less than $200."], "original_party": "Hesai Technology", "reporting_speaker": "CNBC", "tier": "party_statement", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v1"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v1": "86:e2"}, "revises": []}, "86:e3": {"original": {"id": "86:e3", "article": 86, "input_id": "gnews:784c556d2e58ed09dd8aa7fc", "title": "Chinese lidar maker with Nvidia ties accused of being cyber risk for U.S.", "publisher": "cnbc.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "The U.S. House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party, chaired by Rep. John Moolenaar, has proposed legislation that would phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States, arguing the Chinese Communist Party could otherwise exploit a fast-growing, strategically important industry.", "passages": ["The committee has proposed legislation that would phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States, arguing that the Chinese Communist Party could otherwise exploit a fast-growing and strategically important industry."], "verified_passages": ["The committee has proposed legislation that would phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States, arguing that the Chinese Communist Party could otherwise exploit a fast-growing and strategically important industry."], "original_party": "U.S. House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party (Rep. John Moolenaar)", "reporting_speaker": "CNBC", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "87:e1": {"original": {"id": "87:e1", "article": 87, "input_id": "gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9", "title": "A $10,000 Investment in Nvidia at the Start of 2026 Is Up Only 5% -- Here's One Promising Sector That Could Reignite Excitement", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as orbital data centers, called AI1, will use Nvidia chips, and Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers, according to the article. No order size, contract value or delivery date is given.", "passages": ["That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips."], "verified_passages": ["That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips."], "original_party": "SpaceX", "reporting_speaker": "fool.com (Jack Delaney)", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "87:e2": {"original": {"id": "87:e2", "article": 87, "input_id": "gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9", "title": "A $10,000 Investment in Nvidia at the Start of 2026 Is Up Only 5% -- Here's One Promising Sector That Could Reignite Excitement", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "AI start-up Anthropic announced it was renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center, and the announcement disclosed that the data center is powered by more than 220,000 Nvidia GPUs. The item gives no date for the announcement and no contract value.", "passages": ["When the AI start-up Anthropic announced it was renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center, it revealed that the data center is powered by over 220,000 Nvidia graphics processing units (GPUs)."], "verified_passages": ["When the AI start-up Anthropic announced it was renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center, it revealed that the data center is powered by over 220,000 Nvidia graphics processing units (GPUs)."], "original_party": "Anthropic", "reporting_speaker": "fool.com (Jack Delaney)", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v2"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v2": "87:e2"}, "revises": []}, "87:e3": {"original": {"id": "87:e3", "article": 87, "input_id": "gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9", "title": "A $10,000 Investment in Nvidia at the Start of 2026 Is Up Only 5% -- Here's One Promising Sector That Could Reignite Excitement", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "Alphabet has a similar deal to Anthropic's, renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center that gives it access to 110,000 Nvidia GPUs, according to the article. No date or contract value is given.", "passages": ["Alphabet has a similar deal, renting compute capacity that gives it access to 110,000 Nvidia GPUs."], "verified_passages": ["Alphabet has a similar deal, renting compute capacity that gives it access to 110,000 Nvidia GPUs."], "original_party": "Alphabet", "reporting_speaker": "fool.com (Jack Delaney)", "tier": "fact", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v2"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {"v2": "87:e3"}, "revises": []}, "87:e4": {"original": {"id": "87:e4", "article": 87, "input_id": "gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9", "title": "A $10,000 Investment in Nvidia at the Start of 2026 Is Up Only 5% -- Here's One Promising Sector That Could Reignite Excitement", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "Nvidia announced in March [2026] that it will launch a Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future, which will run AI computing directly in space instead of beaming data back to Earth for processing. CEO Jensen Huang, in the company's press release for the announcement, said AI processing across space and ground systems turns orbital data centers into instruments of discovery. No launch date or revenue figure was given.", "passages": ["Also, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future. Instead of beaming data back to Earth for processing, this module will run AI computing directly in space.", "\"AI processing across space and ground systems enables real-time sensing, decision-making, and autonomy, transforming orbital data centers into instruments of discovery and spacecraft into self-navigating systems. With our partners, we're extending Nvidia beyond our planet -- boldly taking intelligence where it's never gone before,\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in the company's press release for the Vera Rubin Space-1 Module announcement."], "verified_passages": ["Also, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future. Instead of beaming data back to Earth for processing, this module will run AI computing directly in space.", "\"AI processing across space and ground systems enables real-time sensing, decision-making, and autonomy, transforming orbital data centers into instruments of discovery and spacecraft into self-navigating systems. With our partners, we're extending Nvidia beyond our planet -- boldly taking intelligence where it's never gone before,\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in the company's press release for the Vera Rubin Space-1 Module announcement."], "original_party": "NVIDIA", "reporting_speaker": "fool.com (Jack Delaney), quoting Jensen Huang", "tier": "party_statement", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": "March", "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "89:e1": {"original": {"id": "89:e1", "article": 89, "input_id": "gnews:cafa45974d83b062f52155e9", "title": "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER Features A 415W TDP, 15% Higher Than RTX 5080, According to Seasonic", "publisher": "wccftech.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "PSU maker Seasonic has silently added the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER to its power-supply wattage calculator, an entry that Videocardz found and that the article treats as a signal Nvidia is proceeding with — rather than cancelling — its RTX 50 SUPER consumer graphics lineup. The article notes the RTX 50 SUPER series had been described over the past months as put on hold or cancelled, and also cites an unnamed well-known insider saying the Super family is 'back on track.' No launch date is given; the article speculates updates later in the year with a possible launch in late 2026 or at CES 2027.", "passages": ["For the past few months, the NVIDIA RTX 50 \"SUPER\" series was said to be essentially put on hold or outright cancelled, but there came a glimmer of hope from a well-known insider who said that the Super family is back on track .", "Now further hints are landing from a PSU manufacturer who has silently added the GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER to its wattage calculator . The last update from Seasonic came last year when it added RTX 5070 Ti SUPER and RTX 5070 SUPER to the same list. Videocardz managed to find this new entry & it looks like there are signals that NVIDIA is going ahead with the lineup instead of canceling it.", "Currently, there's no word on when NVIDIA will launch the RTX 50 SUPER series, and AIBs haven't started talking about these cards yet, not even at the Computex show floor, but it looks like we will get updates in the second half of this year, with a launch possibly taking place in late 2026 or at CES 2027."], "verified_passages": ["For the past few months, the NVIDIA RTX 50 \"SUPER\" series was said to be essentially put on hold or outright cancelled, but there came a glimmer of hope from a well-known insider who said that the Super family is back on track .", "Now further hints are landing from a PSU manufacturer who has silently added the GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER to its wattage calculator . The last update from Seasonic came last year when it added RTX 5070 Ti SUPER and RTX 5070 SUPER to the same list. Videocardz managed to find this new entry & it looks like there are signals that NVIDIA is going ahead with the lineup instead of canceling it.", "Currently, there's no word on when NVIDIA will launch the RTX 50 SUPER series, and AIBs haven't started talking about these cards yet, not even at the Computex show floor, but it looks like we will get updates in the second half of this year, with a launch possibly taking place in late 2026 or at CES 2027."], "original_party": "Seasonic", "reporting_speaker": "wccftech.com (crediting Videocardz for the calculator entry)", "tier": "rumour", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": null, "figures": [], "thesis_ids": [], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [], "links": {}, "revises": []}, "90:e2": {"original": {"id": "90:e2", "article": 90, "input_id": "gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e", "title": "Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "Micron said that 'tight market conditions' in memory could last until 2028, indicating a prolonged memory shortage beyond the near term. Attributed to Micron's recent quarterly announcement as reported by the article; no specific date given.", "passages": ["Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028."], "verified_passages": ["Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028."], "original_party": "Micron Technology", "reporting_speaker": "Keithen Drury, fool.com", "tier": "party_statement", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": null, "evidence_period": "Through 2028", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v5"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}, "members": [{"id": "176:ev9", "article": 176, "input_id": "gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621", "title": "Nvidia Skips New Gaming GPUs, Breaks 30-Year Streak [2026] - https://tech-insider.org/", "publisher": "tech-insider.org", "publication_date": "2026-07-12", "summary": "Micron's leadership described memory market conditions as historically severe on its February 2026 earnings call and has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028; an analysis from Kearney's PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030.", "passages": ["Micron’s own leadership described current conditions as historically severe on the company’s February 2026 earnings call.", "Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 ."], "verified_passages": ["Micron’s own leadership described current conditions as historically severe on the company’s February 2026 earnings call.", "Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 ."], "original_party": "Micron Technology", "reporting_speaker": "tech-insider.org, citing Micron's February 2026 earnings call and Kearney PERLab", "tier": "party_statement", "statement_date": null, "statement_date_text": "February 2026 (Micron earnings call)", "evidence_period": "2027-2028 normalization; shortage possibly persisting to 2030", "figures": [], "thesis_ids": ["v5"], "unresolved_thesis_ids": []}], "links": {"v5": "90:e2"}, "revises": []}, "90:e3": {"original": {"id": "90:e3", "article": 90, "input_id": "gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e", "title": "Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?", "publisher": "fool.com", "publication_date": "2026-07-07", "summary": "The article states that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow. Presented as the author's forecast, not a company figure.", "passages": ["As mentioned, new capacity for many in
... 6866153 bytes omitted ...
 about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\n- e573 [rumour; Reuters (via Business Times), citing the Financial Times; original source Financial Times 2026-07-14; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new \"white list\" of companies that passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14), citing three unnamed people; Reuters could not immediately verify the report and Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment.\n    Quote: NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14). … Reuters could not immediately verify the report. Nvidia did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e522; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e574 [rumour; Reuters (via Business Times), citing the Financial Times; original source Financial Times 2026-07-14; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] Over the past few months NVIDIA has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, and after pressure from Washington has tightened compliance procedures, with staff now visiting customers' data centres, verifying contracts and interviewing end users as part of the checks, according to the Financial Times report citing three unnamed people familiar with the matter.\n    Quote: Over the past few months, the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, the report added, citing three people familiar with the matter. … Nvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\n- e575 [fact; Reuters (via Business Times); original source US Commerce Department ; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia's Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\n    Quote: The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The reference is about $392.7 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The evidence-based forecast remains about $431–433 billion: applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift to consensus for second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue implies roughly $38–40 billion of additional revenue over the reference. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $500 billion. Strong reported growth and Q2 guidance support the run-rate, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility and announced AI-cloud capacities span periods or lack a FY2027 shipment s\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 5. The separate Morgan Stanley forecast also exceeds $1 trillion, though it is for broader AI infrastructure rather than explicitly data-center capex. The 2026 plan of $650 billion followed by a 2027 forec\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Flat (rung 3): no market consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Persistent tight supply through 2027 supports further increases, but the sharp 2026 contract-price run-up makes the comparison base much higher; together thes\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is the reported status quo: no public Chinese authorization, which is rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence does not establish that the rung-1 event has already happened: the specific reports describe a plan to let selected firms buy limited quantities, with fi\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus, so the market reference is a proxy: approximately $392.7 billion of FY2027 total-revenue consensus multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%, or roughly $361 billion. That reference is in rung 2. The current total-revenue dependency estimate is $431–433 billion; applying the same 92% mix implies about $397–398 billion of Data Center revenue, also rung 2. Q1 results and Q2 guidance support the run-rate, while SemiAnalysis's quantified H2 compute upside is already reflected in the dependency estimate and is not added again. New Firmus off-take and investment evidence, plus the AWS deployment plan, supports demand but is multi-period and does not establish FY2027 recognized revenue; project delays and other demand/price risks o\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "cd0051a17e1ef4909d066043052ba8fa0e93f283def16342d8ebce6e53c2598a": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v3 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 77%. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 75% but below 77%. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 73% but below 75%, compared with the article's 75% reference level. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 71% but below 73%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is below 71%.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\n- e88 [fact; Neil Patel (Motley Fool); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:068a24e2743bab750f43f048 2026-07-04] Nvidia's gross margin was 74.9% during Q1 (Q1 FY2027), which the article attributes in part to the company's pricing power.\n    Quote: Nvidia's pricing power plays a role here, as its gross margin was 74.9% during Q1.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e167; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e123 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200 while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\n    Quote: The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. … As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent o\n- e125 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source MarketScale ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, attributing the pressure to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand.\n    Quote: Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\n- e139 [party_statement; Zacks (via TradingView); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA guided second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth, and guided a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%, up from 72.7% a year earlier.\n    Quote: Management also expects the momentum to continue. For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth. The company also expects a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%, up from 72.7% a year ago, reflecting healthy pricing power and operational efficiency.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e160, e320, e360, e366, e440, e459, e472, e484, e513, e526; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e160 [party_statement; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%, as cited by 24/7 Wall St.\n    Quote: Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e167 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] In a comparison with Micron, the author cited Nvidia’s latest reported quarter as showing revenue growth of 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of roughly 66%, and said Nvidia still earns more dollars.\n    Quote: Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e168 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\n    Quote: For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e169 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Apple ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\n    Quote: Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e125 (); both are in force: The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, attributing the pressure to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand.\n- e226 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury, fool.com ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow. Presented as the author's forecast, not a company figure.\n    Quote: As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. … That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e384; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e289 [third_party_view; Investing.com via Yahoo Finance UK; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] BofA analyst Vivek Arya said investors overstate HBM cost pressure while underestimating Nvidia's pricing power, scale and supply-chain commitments, and BofA expects Nvidia's gross margins to remain around the mid-70% range.\n    Quote: On margins, Arya said investors \"overstate HBM cost pressure while underestimating NVDA's pricing power, scale, and $119 billion of supply-chain commitments.\" … BofA expects gross margins to remain around the mid-70% range.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e302; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e290 [third_party_view; Investing.com via Yahoo Finance UK; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] BofA analyst Vivek Arya estimated that HBM content per rack may rise by approximately $0.2–0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while rack pricing could increase by $2–3 million, driven by upgrades across compute, networking and software.\n    Quote: While HBM content per rack may rise by approximately $0.2-0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, Arya noted that rack pricing could increase by $2-3 million, driven by upgrades across compute, networking and software.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e301; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e301 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA, in a report summarized by moomoo.com, estimated that HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million in the move from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 million for Vera Rubin — BofA's argument that higher rack pricing offsets HBM inflation.\n    Quote: The key bear case is HBM inflation. BofA does not deny the cost pressure, but argues the market is missing the offsetting pricing power. The report estimates HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 millio\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e290; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e302 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA expects Nvidia's gross margin to stay around the mid-70% range rather than collapse, because it judges the increase in HBM cost per rack to be more than offset by the increase in total rack pricing.\n    Quote: That math is why BofA expects gross margin to stay around the mid-70% range rather than collapse.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e289; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e311 [third_party_view; Russell Brandom, TechCrunch; original source High-bandwidth memory makers, including Micron 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] TechCrunch reported that makers of high-bandwidth memory (HBM) such as Micron have been able to raise prices tenfold over the past year, because HBM demand from data-center buildouts is growing faster than anyone can scale supply. The figure is the journalist's account of the market, not a company-disclosed price.\n    Quote: They build high-bandwidth memory chips — specialized components designed to move data in and out of processors as fast as possible — which have been getting incrementally better for 20 years. Without the chips or the companies changing too much, the service they provide suddenly became very valuable — and since demand is growing faster than anyone can scale up supply, they have been able to increa\n- e314 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Vivek Arya (Bank of America) ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya, in a note this week, cited gross margin pressure from higher memory costs among the concerns fueling Nvidia's sell-off.\n    Quote: Several concerns are fueling the sell-off, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week. They include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\n- e317 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-05-20; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia reported first-quarter fiscal 2027 results (quarter ended April 26, 2026) on May 20: record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year; GAAP net income of $58.32 billion, up 211% YOY; non-GAAP diluted EPS of $1.87, up 140% from the prior-year period and above analyst estimates; and non-GAAP gross margin of 75%, up from 60.8% a year earlier.\n    Quote: Nvidia delivered another exceptional quarter when it reported first-quarter fiscal 2027 results on May 20, further cementing its leadership in the rapidly growing AI infrastructure market.\nFor the quarter ended April 26, Nvidia posted record revenue of $81.6 billion, an 85% year-over-year (YOY) increase, while net income jumped 211% YOY to $58.32 billion. … On a non-GAAP basis, earnings per share \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e320 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-05-20; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia guided second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and non-GAAP gross margin to about 75%; the guidance assumes no contribution from China Data Center compute revenue because of ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\n    Quote: Additionally, Nvidia issued another optimistic outlook for the second quarter of fiscal 2027, forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%. The guidance assumes no contribution from China Data Center compute revenue due to ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e358 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, Bloomberg (via latimes.com); original source Jeran Wittenstein, Bloomberg (via latimes.com) ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] The article reports that prices for high-bandwidth memory chips have been soaring in 2026 and credits this with driving Micron Technology's share gains in the semiconductor index; no price level or contract price comparison is given.\n    Quote: The chip index is being led by Micron, which is benefiting from soaring prices for high-bandwidth memory chips.\n- e369 [fact; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's revenue grew 85.23% year over year at a 75.0% non-GAAP gross margin, per the article's latest-reported figures.\n    Quote: For a business growing revenue 85.23% year over year at 75.0% non-GAAP gross margins, that is cheap on any historical comparison.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e374 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (David Moadel); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin was 75% in Q1 FY2027, as reported in its quarterly results.\n    Quote: Non-GAAP gross margin came in at 75%\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e471, e515; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e416 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org, citing PCWorld; original source NVIDIA product margins, per reporting cited by PCWorld ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Reporting cited by PCWorld puts NVIDIA's AI accelerator profit margins near 65%, against roughly 40% for consumer gaming GPUs, a gap the item uses to explain why scarce memory is being allocated to AI products rather than GeForce cards.\n    Quote: According to reporting cited by PCWorld, Nvidia’s AI accelerators carry profit margins near 65% , compared with roughly 40% for consumer gaming GPUs. … the underlying margin math, roughly 65% for AI accelerators versus roughly 40% for gaming GPUs, does not change just because gamers are unhappy about it.\n- e424 [fact; jpost.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:c14249088492921c802c839a 2026-07-12] jpost reported that Micron's gross profit margin jumped to 84.9% in its third quarter from just 39% the prior year, a move it attributes heavily to high prices for high-bandwidth memory (HBM) chips.\n    Quote: Micron's meteoric run was heavily influenced by high prices for high-bandwidth memory chips, which jumped the company's gross profit margin in the third quarter to 84.9%, compared to just 39% the previous year.\n- e447 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] Nvidia maintained its gross margin outlook in the mid-70% range; the item does not specify the period or whether the measure is GAAP or non-GAAP.\n    Quote: maintained its gross margin outlook in the mid-70% range\n- e471 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA's Q1 FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin was 75%, up from 60.8% a year earlier, a comparison the article attributes to its CUDA software moat.\n    Quote: That software moat is why NVIDIA’s Q1 FY27 non-GAAP gross margin printed at 75%, expanded from 60.8% a year earlier.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e374; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e472 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion at a 75% non-GAAP gross margin.\n    Quote: But NVIDIA guided $91 billion for Q2 FY27 anyway, with a 75% non-GAAP gross margin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e507 [rumour; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source SK Hynix (per an unnamed report); Korean analysts (margin-gap estimate) ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] According to a report cited by the article, SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM's; Korean analysts pegged that margin gap at more than 15 points. The item reports this as a margin story rather than a demand signal, and no volume or capacity figure is given.\n    Quote: The previous big move we wrote about was 21 days ago when the stock dropped 3.7% after a report that South Korea's SK Hynix is slowing its high-bandwidth memory (HBM) expansion rattled the AI-chip complex. … The headline sounds bearish for AI, but the underlying report is a margin story, not a demand story. SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM,\n- e508 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Samsung; SK Hynix (reported DRAM ASP increases) ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] The article reports that all three memory makers are running the memory market tight, keeping pricing power with sellers, citing Samsung's flagged 146% jump in DRAM average selling prices in Q1 and SK Hynix's mid-60% increase.\n    Quote: The more accurate read is that all three memory makers are running the market tight (Samsung flagged a 146% DRAM ASP jump in Q1, SK Hynix mid-60%), keeping pricing power with sellers.\n- e515 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] 247wallst.com states that NVIDIA is compounding revenue north of 85% year over year with 75% gross margins, without specifying the period for the margin figure.\n    Quote: At $203.53 against forward EPS of $8, Nvidia’s forward P/E 25x is not expensive for a company compounding revenue north of 85% YoY with 75% gross margins.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e374; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e558 [fact; EBC (ebc.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia has committed $119 billion to secure its supply chain, a commitment the article ties to rising high-bandwidth memory (HBM) costs, which it says are raising concerns about Nvidia's long-term gross margins.\n    Quote: Supply Chain Commitments: Nvidia has committed a staggering $119 billion to secure its supply chain, but the soaring costs of HBM are raising alarms about the company's long-term gross margins.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e151; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The reference is about $392.7 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The evidence-based forecast remains about $431–433 billion: applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift to consensus for second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue implies roughly $38–40 billion of additional revenue over the reference. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $500 billion. Strong reported growth and Q2 guidance support the run-rate, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility and announced AI-cloud capacities span periods or lack a FY2027 shipment s\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 5. The separate Morgan Stanley forecast also exceeds $1 trillion, though it is for broader AI infrastructure rather than explicitly data-center capex. The 2026 plan of $650 billion followed by a 2027 forec\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Flat (rung 3): no market consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Persistent tight supply through 2027 supports further increases, but the sharp 2026 contract-price run-up makes the comparison base much higher; together thes\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is the reported status quo: no public Chinese authorization, which is rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence does not establish that the rung-1 event has already happened: the specific reports describe a plan to let selected firms buy limited quantities, with fi\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The implied FY2027 margin is approximately 75.0%, at rung 2's 75% lower boundary. Q1 FY2027 reported non-GAAP gross margin was 75%, and Q2 guidance is also about 75%; the material cost evidence weighs against assuming expansion, but BofA's quantified rack-pricing comparison supports those costs being substantially offset. This is a forecast, not a claim that the full-year result is established. The revenue dependency is consistent with strong demand and scale, but does not itself determine gross margin.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "23cfc5c515646637bfbaf52f3b5ee183696fc648e25d0bb71fd4d4e6d490c0ec": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v10 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 non-GAAP operating margin\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What non-GAAP operating margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 non-GAAP operating margin is at least 68%. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 non-GAAP operating margin is at least 65% and below 68%. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 non-GAAP operating margin is at least 62% and below 65%. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 non-GAAP operating margin is at least 58% and below 62%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 non-GAAP operating margin is below 58%.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\n- e123 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200 while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\n    Quote: The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. … As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent o\n- e167 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] In a comparison with Micron, the author cited Nvidia’s latest reported quarter as showing revenue growth of 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of roughly 66%, and said Nvidia still earns more dollars.\n    Quote: Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e88; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e168 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\n    Quote: For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e169 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Apple ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\n    Quote: Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e125 (); both are in force: The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, attributing the pressure to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand.\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 5. The separate Morgan Stanley forecast also exceeds $1 trillion, though it is for broader AI infrastructure rather than explicitly data-center capex. The 2026 plan of $650 billion followed by a 2027 forec\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Flat (rung 3): no market consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Persistent tight supply through 2027 supports further increases, but the sharp 2026 contract-price run-up makes the comparison base much higher; together thes\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is the reported status quo: no public Chinese authorization, which is rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence does not establish that the rung-1 event has already happened: the specific reports describe a plan to let selected firms buy limited quantities, with fi\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: No FY2027 consensus or company guidance is supplied; the nearest observable reference is NVIDIA's latest reported operating margin of roughly 66%, which sits in rung 2. The higher memory content and memory-cost pressure weigh against carrying that margin through unchanged, while the AI-capex demand backdrop supports it; the evidence does not quantify a consolidated margin hit large enough to put the forecast below 65%.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "a308fbe8ef6785f328c2aa89f8b75e21a9127c6a83a4b2ab967d34f4eabeea95": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 28 items]\n[Not shown: 2 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e1 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced it is introducing a new business model under which AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model; under the partnership the AI clouds sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services while NVIDIA earns both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. No commitment amounts, counterparties or credit-support sizes are disclosed.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-pow\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e25, e32, e37, e63, e80, e89, e153, e211, e265; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3 [fact; TradingKey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01] NVIDIA invested $5 billion of equity in Intel (NASDAQ: INTC), a transaction TradingKey's July 1, 2026 article treats as one of the two central catalysts of the Intel investment case and describes elsewhere as an equity commitment; the item gives no date for the investment itself.\n    Quote: Intel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. … Add to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's m\n- e13 [fact; moomoo.com (citing NVIDIA official statement/press release); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA published a statement on its official website on July 1 announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. NVIDIA said it will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream; a NVIDIA spokesperson confirmed the program, internally called the 'AI Compute Partnership.'\n    Quote: On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients. … According to\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e14 [rumour; moomoo.com (citing The Information; arrangement attributed to unnamed executives); original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price; in return NVIDIA will receive a percentage of the providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e17 [fact; moomoo.com (citing NVIDIA regulatory filing); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\n    Quote: According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\n- e18 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave's unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\n    Quote: In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion.\n- e19 [rumour; moomoo.com (citing prior reporting by The Information); original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio, an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion. The $500 billion is the estimat