ucture Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs; if a partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors, and it collects standard product revenue plus a usage-linked cut of cloud revenue. Nvidia described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\" The specific revenue-share percentage was not disclosed and Nvidia declined to offer further details beyond the blog post.\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \"AI Compute Partnership,\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate … The backstop mechanism works by Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, effectively de-risking lender exposure and \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e89 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] NVIDIA unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model under which AI cloud operators can procure NVIDIA infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold on that capacity; NVIDIA receives its standard product revenue from the infrastructure sale plus a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity. No dollar size or term for the credit support is disclosed.\n    Quote: Nvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers. … Under this financing structure, 