ference shares that are designed to convert to ordinary equity at the initial public offering later this year. The raise is set to almost double the neocloud's equity value to $15.5 billion ahead of its ASX listing, per AFR Street Talk.\n    Quote: Nasdaq-listed Nvidia has emerged as one of the biggest investors in Firmus Technologies’ $US2 billion ($2.9 billion) equity raising, which is set to almost double the neocloud’s equity value to $15.5 billion before its ASX listing. … Street Talk can reveal that the $US4.9 trillion chip developer has put up its hand for about $US500 million ($721 million) worth of Firmus’ preference shares, which a\n- e266 [fact; Futuriom, reporting NVIDIA's announcement; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA stated that two AI cloud providers have already signed on for its new revenue-sharing/credit-support model, and that Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\n    Quote: NVIDIA states that two AI cloud providers have already signed on for the new deal: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e14 (); both are in force: The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price; in return NVIDIA will receive a percentage of the providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\n- e267 [fact; Futuriom, reporting NVIDIA's announcement; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Firmus, based in Indonesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs, as one of the two AI cloud providers NVIDIA said have signed on for its new revenue-sharing/credit-support model.\n    Quote: Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e316 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Vivek Arya (Bank of America) ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya, in a note this week, cited unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends among the concerns fueling Nvidia's sell-off.\n    Quote: Several concerns are fueling the sell-off, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week. They include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The reference is about $392.7 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The evidence-based forecast remains about $431–433 billion: applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift to consensus for second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue implies roughly $38–40 billion of additional revenue over the reference. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $500 billion. Strong reported growth and Q2 guidance support the run-rate, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility and announced AI-cloud capacities span periods or lack a FY2027 shipment s\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 5. The separate Morgan Stanley forecast also exceeds $1 trillion, though it is for broader AI infrastructure rather than explicitly data-center capex. The 2026 plan of $650 billion followed by a 2027 forec\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Flat (rung 3): no market consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Persistent tight supply through 2027 supports further increases, but the sharp 2026 contract-price run-up makes the comparison base much higher; together thes\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-13; reasons: The reference is the reported status quo: no public Chinese authorization, which is rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence does not establish that the rung-1 event has already happened: the specific reports describe a plan to let selected firms buy limited quantities, with fi\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The reference is unplaced: no aggregate market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan for the FY2027 year-end total is supplied. The implied estimate is about $38 billion, below rung 5's $40 billion boundary: carry forward roughly $22 billion of non-Firmus commitments and backstops, and estimate Firmus at about $16 billion by applying a 25% execution haircut to the roughly $21.1 billion six-year backstop implied by 170,000 GPUs at SemiAnalysis's low-end $2.36/GPU-hour benchmark. The haircut reflects that 170,000 is a stated maximum buildout, not volume shown to be in hand by year-end. Sharon AI's $4.88 billion backstop is disclosed, while the newer reports of additional neocloud and data-center backstops are unquantified, so they support upside but do not establish enough to put the fo\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "c90b33e49dc10648ad7a0d3b0fb2eaf3ca688b4c18f052ac631d94a198ac1b34": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v5 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \"Server DRAM and HBM contract prices, calendar 2027 against 2026\", 2027, due 2028-01-31): How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Up 30% or more: 2027 average contract prices at least 30% above 2026. / rung 2 Up 10% to 30%: at least 10% and below 30% above 2026. / rung 3 Flat: within 10% of 2026 either way. / rung 4 Down 10% to 30%: at least 10% and below 30% under 2026. / rung 5 Down 30% or more: 30% or more under 2026.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 36 items]\n- e62 [fact; The Diplomat (Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander); original source Samsung and SK Hynix (as reported) ; from gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5 2026-07-02] Samsung and SK Hynix, which the article says produce most of Nvidia's memory chips, are pursuing massive expansions to meet surging chip demand, and both are key partners in South Korea's 'Yongin Mega-cluster' semiconductor fab and AI-factory complex. The item gives no size or date for the memory capacity additions.\n    Quote: Near Taiwan, South Korea – another major Nvidia manufacturing hub – faces a similar challenge. Samsung and SK Hynix, the two local giants that produce most of Nvidia’s memory chips, are pursuing massive expansions to meet surging chip demand, with little attention to the resulting strain on electricity and emissions. Both companies are key partners in the government’s “Yongin Mega-cluster” plan , \n- e98 [third_party_view; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com ; from gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2 2026-07-05] wccftech reports that the DRAM crisis is intensifying in 2026 and that graphics-card prices continue to rise across all commodity memory, including the GDDR7 used in NVIDIA's RTX 50 GPUs. The outlet states that DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year and long-term agreements and are committing billions of dollars to new fabrication facilities aimed mainly at meeting AI-driven demand, and it believes the shortage will not end soon and is unlikely to relieve the consumer market.\n    Quote: As the DRAM crisis intensifies in 2026, graphics card prices continue to go up. We see some weird prioritization of specific models with higher capacity, where the increased margins lie. All new RTX 50 GPUs come with GDDR7 memory, which is the best you can get, and the problem is that all kinds of commodity memory are expensive these days. … And since DRAM manufacturers have locked in multi-year a\n- e123 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200 while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\n    Quote: The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. … As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent o\n- e125 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source MarketScale ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, attributing the pressure to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand.\n    Quote: Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\n- e148 [fact; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source Micron ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Micron reported fiscal Q3 revenue rose 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra cited multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements meant to lock in durability, per 24/7 Wall St.\n    Quote: Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e168, e386; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e149 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source Micron ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Micron forecast adjusted gross margin of 86% for its next quarter, a level the article notes is above the peak gross margin NVIDIA has ever achieved.\n    Quote: Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\n- e150 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Some estimates cited by 24/7 Wall St. put half of all data-center spending going toward memory in 2027.\n    Quote: Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\n- e168 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth); GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion; gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%; and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%.\n    Quote: For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e169 [fact; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Apple ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\n    Quote: Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e125 (); both are in force: The article reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, attributing the pressure to constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand.\n- e207 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence report warned that a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms such as SMIC, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, and that bottlenecks are shifting from computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips.\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits. … The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e287; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e225 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron said that 'tight market conditions' in memory could last until 2028, indicating a prolonged memory shortage beyond the near term. Attributed to Micron's recent quarterly announcement as reported by the article; no specific date given.\n    Quote: Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e417; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e226 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury, fool.com ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow. Presented as the author's forecast, not a company figure.\n    Quote: As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. … That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e384; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e287 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Fortune); original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence report said a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms such as SMIC, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to benefit, as bottlenecks shift from raw computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer.\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. with ChangXin Memory Technologies Inc. poised to reap the benefits. The bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e207; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e311 [third_party_view; Russell Brandom, TechCrunch; original source High-bandwidth memory makers, including Micron 2026-07-09; from gnews:3ea4e94c707ece2db2d7caec 2026-07-09] TechCrunch reported that makers of high-bandwidth memory (HBM) such as Micron have been able to raise prices tenfold over the past year, because HBM demand from data-center buildouts is growing faster than anyone can scale supply. The figure is the journalist's account of the market, not a company-disclosed price.\n    Quote: They build high-bandwidth memory chips — specialized components designed to move data in and out of processors as fast as possible — which have been getting incrementally better for 20 years. Without the chips or the companies changing too much, the service they provide suddenly became very valuable — and since demand is growing faster than anyone can scale up supply, they have been able to increa\n- e314 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Vivek Arya (Bank of America) ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya, in a note this week, cited gross margin pressure from higher memory costs among the concerns fueling Nvidia's sell-off.\n    Quote: Several concerns are fueling the sell-off, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week. They include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\n- e347 [third_party_view; Bruce Gil (Gizmodo); original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:fe9210b7b43c51e0939ecbbb 2026-07-09] Tom's Hardware reported earlier in 2026 that large AI data-center projects such as OpenAI's Stargate are calling for hundreds of thousands more DRAM wafers per month, amounting to roughly 40% of global DRAM output, reflecting data-center-driven demand pressure on DRAM supply.\n    Quote: Tom’s Hardware reported earlier this year that huge data center projects like OpenAI’s Stargate are calling for hundreds of thousands more DRAM wafers per month, amounting to roughly 40% of global DRAM output.\n- e348 [fact; Bruce Gil (Gizmodo); original source Micron Technology (with Samsung and SK Hynix) ; from gnews:fe9210b7b43c51e0939ecbbb 2026-07-09] In response to increased AI demand, memory makers SK Hynix, Samsung and Micron have increasingly prioritized data-center customers over consumer electronics companies, with Micron deciding to wind down its consumer brand Crucial to focus on data-center demand.\n    Quote: In response to increased demand from AI companies, memory makers like SK Hynix, Samsung, and Micron have increasingly prioritized data center customers over consumer electronics companies. Micron even decided to wind down its consumer business brand , Crucial, to focus on data center demand.\n- e358 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, Bloomberg (via latimes.com); original source Jeran Wittenstein, Bloomberg (via latimes.com) ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] The article reports that prices for high-bandwidth memory chips have been soaring in 2026 and credits this with driving Micron Technology's share gains in the semiconductor index; no price level or contract price comparison is given.\n    Quote: The chip index is being led by Micron, which is benefiting from soaring prices for high-bandwidth memory chips.\n- e379 [fact; theverge.com, citing The Associated Press and CNN; original source SK Hynix 2026-07-10; from gnews:3839887e5ca53a0cb0e7c91e 2026-07-10] SK Hynix, described as one of the world's biggest memory-chip suppliers and a major supplier of RAM/HBM to AI data centers, launched on Wall Street on Friday (the item's publication day), opening at $170 per share and raising $26.5 billion, which the article says surpasses Alibaba's record as the largest debut of a foreign company, citing The Associated Press and CNN. The item does not state how the proceeds will be used.\n    Quote: As the AI boom boosts demand for RAM, SK Hynix — one of the world’s biggest suppliers of memory chips — launched on Wall Street Friday. The South Korean chipmaker opened at $170 per share and raised $26.5 billion, surpassing Alibaba’s record as the largest debut of a foreign company, according to reports from The Associated Press and CNN .\n- e380 [third_party_view; theverge.com; original source Counterpoint (market data provider) ; from gnews:3839887e5ca53a0cb0e7c91e 2026-07-10] According to Counterpoint data cited by The Verge, as of June 2026 SK Hynix held 29 percent of the global DRAM market, Samsung 38 percent and Micron 22 percent — a three-supplier concentration. The shares are for the global DRAM market overall, not HBM specifically.\n    Quote: As of June 2026, SK Hynix makes up 29 percent of the global DRAM market, with Samsung at 38 percent and Micron at 22 percent, according to data from Counterpoint .\n- e381 [party_statement; Bloomberg (reported by theverge.com); original source SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won ; from gnews:3839887e5ca53a0cb0e7c91e 2026-07-10] SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won said in June 2026 that the company (SK Hynix's parent group, per the article's framing) plans to ramp up its memory chip capacity over the next five years to address a memory shortage that could last until 2030, as reported by Bloomberg. No capacity volumes or amounts are given, and the ramp is a plan, not completed capacity.\n    Quote: SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won said in June that the company plans to ramp up its memory chip capacity over the next five years to address a shortage that could last until 2030, Bloomberg reports .\n- e382 [fact; Kristina Partsinevelos (CNBC), reported by 24/7 Wall St. (Thomas Richmond); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] SK Hynix completed a roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share, described as the second-largest share sale ever (trailing only SpaceX's Nasdaq debut), giving U.S. investors direct access to the memory supplier central to NVIDIA's chips.\n    Quote: During a July 10 CNBC segment , Kristina Partsinevelos framed SK Hynix’s roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share as a defining moment for the AI supply chain. … The offering ranks as the second-largest share sale ever, trailing only SpaceX’s NASDAQ debut last month.\n- e383 [third_party_view; Kristina Partsinevelos (CNBC); original source Counterpoint Research (market-share estimate, cited by CNBC/24-7 Wall St.) 2026-07-10; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Per Counterpoint Research, SK Hynix holds roughly 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, while Micron and Samsung each hold roughly 21%, as cited by CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos.\n    Quote: SK Hynix controls 58% of the HBM market, and its $26.5B Nasdaq debut finally gives U.S. investors direct access to NVIDIA's most critical chip supplier. … SK Hynix is the world leader in high bandwidth memory . The memory that feeds AI chips with roughly 58% of that market. Micron and Samsung competitors split roughly 21% each … as per Counterpoint Research.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e433; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e384 [third_party_view; Kristina Partsinevelos (CNBC); original source Kristina Partsinevelos (CNBC) 2026-07-10; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos said none of the new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, which she said keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now, and that the memory shortage is likely to persist into 2027.\n    Quote: “None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said. … For now, the memory shortage is likely to persist into 2027, index inclusion should act as a mechanical bid for the stock, and the three players controlling roughly 95% of memory production remain in a pricing environment they have not enjoyed \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e226; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e385 [third_party_view; Kristina Partsinevelos (CNBC); original source Kristina Partsinevelos (CNBC) / SK Hynix statements 2026-07-10; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos reported SK Hynix's revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, that the company is committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years (primarily in South Korea), and that roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\n    Quote: Partsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea. … A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\n- e386 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (Thomas Richmond); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Micron reported Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%.\n    Quote: Micron posted Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, with non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e387 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (Thomas Richmond); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Micron guided the following quarter (Q4 FY2026) to revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion.\n    Quote: Guidance for the following quarter calls for revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e227; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e388 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (Thomas Richmond); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f 2026-07-10] Micron said its HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and that HBM4E is in development with volume production expected in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Micron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027.\n- e414 [fact; CNBC (January 2026), cited by tech-insider.org; original source Samsung, SK Hynix and Micron ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix and Micron have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output to AI data-center customers under long-term contracts, according to CNBC reporting from January 2026 cited in the item, as hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta and Amazon buy up high-bandwidth memory as fast as it can be produced.\n    Quote: Hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon are buying up high-bandwidth memory as fast as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC’s reporting from January 2026. … The cause is a global memory shortage: Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have reportedly sol\n- e415 [fact; tech-insider.org, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware (pricing tracker) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Tom's Hardware has tracked DRAM contract pricing climbing by roughly 60% to more than 90% quarter-over-quarter at different points in 2026, depending on segment and tracker, even as some consumer retail prices cooled as buyers hit affordability limits.\n    Quote: Tom’s Hardware has tracked DRAM contract pricing climbing by anywhere from roughly 60% to more than 90% quarter-over-quarter at different points in 2026, depending on the segment and the tracker consulted, even as some consumer retail prices have started to cool slightly as buyers hit their affordability ceiling.\n- e417 [party_statement; tech-insider.org, citing Micron's February 2026 earnings call and Kearney PERLab; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Micron's leadership described memory market conditions as historically severe on its February 2026 earnings call and has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028; an analysis from Kearney's PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030.\n    Quote: Micron’s own leadership described current conditions as historically severe on the company’s February 2026 earnings call. … Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney’s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e225; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e424 [fact; jpost.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:c14249088492921c802c839a 2026-07-12] jpost reported that Micron's gross profit margin jumped to 84.9% in its third quarter from just 39% the prior year, a move it attributes heavily to high prices for high-bandwidth memory (HBM) chips.\n    Quote: Micron's meteoric run was heavily influenced by high prices for high-bandwidth memory chips, which jumped the company's gross profit margin in the third quarter to 84.9%, compared to just 39% the previous year.\n- e432 [fact; Adria Cimino, fool.com; original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix, which the article identifies as Nvidia's main HBM/memory partner, reported revenue growth of 198% to about $35 billion (a record level) in its recent reported quarter, with net income up 398% and an operating margin of 72%.\n    Quote: In the recent quarter, for example, the company reported revenue growth of 198% to about $35 billion dollars, a record level. Net income soared 398%, and operating margin came in at 72%.\n- e433 [third_party_view; Counterpoint Research (cited by fool.com); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] According to Counterpoint Research, SK Hynix held 58% of the global HBM market as of the end of the first quarter.\n    Quote: SK Hynix held 58% share globally of the HBM market as of the end of the first quarter, according to Counterpoint Research.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e383; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e507 [rumour; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source SK Hynix (per an unnamed report); Korean analysts (margin-gap estimate) ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] According to a report cited by the article, SK Hynix is