specific quantities and use cases, according to the item.\n    Quote: The stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e254; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e375 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (David Moadel); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion, an outlook that assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China; the article notes that a policy thaw on H200 exports to China could therefore be additional upside to that guidance.\n    Quote: Non-GAAP gross margin came in at 75%, and management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion. Notably, that outlook assumed zero Data Center compute revenue from China, meaning any policy thaw on H200 exports could represent additional upside.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e367; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e442 [fact; barchart.com; original source Beijing (Chinese government) ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Beijing has encouraged Chinese companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, creating additional pressure on Nvidia's growth in one of its largest overseas markets, according to the article.\n    Quote: Beijing has also encouraged companies to reduce purchases of foreign AI processors, creating additional pressure on Nvidia's long-term growth in one of its largest overseas markets.\n- e456 [fact; fool.com (Stefon Walters); original source U.S. government ; from gnews:f4eb5206f2995f5dbb86c5e9 2026-07-13] The U.S. has already implemented strict export bans on certain chips to China, the article states, describing this regulatory backdrop as a reason NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang's condemnation of chip smuggling is aimed at staying in the U.S. government's good graces.\n    Quote: The U.S. has already implemented strict export bans on certain chips to China, so Huang's taking this stance is a way to stay in the good graces of the U.S. government and avoid further crackdowns or potential fines.\n- e463 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] NVIDIA's Q2 guidance assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue, according to 24/7 Wall St.\n    Quote: NVIDIA leans on scale and CUDA gravity, but assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue in Q2 guidance, a headwind Broadcom does not carry to the same degree.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e367; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e473 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped no H20 compute products to China in Q1 FY2027, and its Q2 FY2027 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: There were no H20 compute products shipped to China in Q1 FY27, and the Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e367; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e490 [party_statement; CNBC, reporting Kessler's remarks at a congressional hearing; original source Jeffery Kessler, Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security (U.S. government) 2026-07-14; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said at a congressional hearing on Tuesday, July 14, 2026, that \"very few\" of Nvidia's H200 AI chips have been shipped to China and Hong Kong, describing it as a very small quantity of chips and saying that very few shipments against licences for H200s and equivalents have taken place.\n    Quote: A top U.S. trade official said on Tuesday that \"very few\" of Nvidia's H200 AI chips have been shipped to China and Hong Kong. … \"The bottom line is very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It's a very small quantity of chips,\" Under Secretary of Commerce for Industry and Security Jeffery Kessler said at a congressional hearing.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e506, e557; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e491 [fact; CNBC; original source President Donald Trump / U.S. government ; from gnews:9d898f2bf43ed52a98e1587a 2026-07-14] President Donald Trump said in December that the U.S. government would approve China sales of Nvidia's H200 AI chip in exchange for a 25% cut, and licences for the chips were issued earlier this year (before the July 14, 2026 article).\n    Quote: In December, President Donald Trump said that the U.S. government would approve China sales of the H200 AI chip in exchange for a 25% cut. Licenses for the chips, which some in the administration say can be used for military purposes, were issued earlier this year .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e506 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com), reporting the official's testimony to Congress; original source U.S. Commerce Department official ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] A top U.S. Commerce Department official told Congress that Nvidia has begun shipping its H200 AI chips to China, saying the number of H200 chips shipped to China so far was \"very few\" but that the shipments have commenced; the article also reports a U.S. official confirmed the company has begun shipping H200 chips to China.\n    Quote: Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 4% in the afternoon session after Morgan Stanley reiterated its \"overweight\" rating on the stock, and a U.S. official confirmed the company has begun shipping its powerful H200 AI chips to China. … Separately, a top U.S. commerce official told Congress that while the number of H200 chips shipped to China so far was \"very few,\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e490; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e54 (); both are in force: The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for H200 exports.\n- e514 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, a condition 247wallst.com describes as a continued overhang on the outlook.\n    Quote: Guidance also excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, a real overhang.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e492; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e523 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Jowi Morales); original source U.S. government (President Donald Trump) ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] President Donald Trump in December 2025 reversed course and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region.\n    Quote: President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\n- e529 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91 billion, a guidance that assumes zero China Data Center compute revenue.\n    Quote: NVIDIA guided Q2 to $91 billion in revenue, which assumes zero China Data Center compute.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e367; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e532 [rumour; Vandita Jadeja (247wallst.com); original source 247wallst.com ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] The article states that there are no NVIDIA H20 shipments to China.\n    Quote: No H20 shipments to China\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e368; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e540 [rumour; Reuters; original source U.S. Department of Commerce (Bureau of Industry and Security) export licensing ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] Reuters, citing documents and two unnamed sources, reported that ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra have received U.S. approval to purchase Nvidia's H200 AI chips, and that Zhuhai Hengqin Yunxiang Zhisheng Network Technology, a subsidiary of cloud computing company Kingsoft, has been cleared to use some AMD chips that rival the H200. The three firms had not previously been reported to have U.S. clearance. Nvidia, AMD, ZTE, Kingsoft and China's Ministry of Commerce did not respond to requests for comment.\n    Quote: A unit of telecoms gear maker ZTE Corp and two other Chinese firms are among the latest entities to receive U.S. approval to purchase ‌advanced AI chips from Nvidia and AMD, according to documents and two sources ‌familiar with the matter. … ZTE Kangxun Telecom and server maker Maginfra have been permitted to purchase Nvidia's H200 chips, while Zhuhai Hengqin Yunxiang Zhisheng Network Technology, \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e554, e555; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e541 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed Chinese cloud computing firms (as reported by sources) ; from gnews:24268bfaab6ef1d7876b188c 2026-07-14] Unnamed sources told Reuters that some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain Nvidia H200 chips, which the sources said indicates some progress in import reviews by Chinese authorities. No authorization from Chinese authorities is reported as issued.\n    Quote: However, some Chinese cloud firms have recently told partners and clients they may soon be able to obtain H200 chips, the sources said, indicating some progress in import reviews by Chinese authorities.\n- e554 [fact; Reuters (as reported by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government (export approval) 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported on 2026-07-14 that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-generation H200 'Hopper' AI chips. ZTE joins a group of roughly 10 Chinese companies with U.S. clearance for such purchases, which also includes Alibaba, Tencent, ByteDance and JD.com.\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \"Hopper\" chips. … ZTE joins a club that counts Alibaba, Tencent, ByteDance, and JD.com among the roughly 10-strong group of Chinese companies with U.S. clearance for those purchases.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e540; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e556 [third_party_view; Reuters (as reported by Tom's Hardware); original source Chinese authorities (stance), reported via Reuters ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported there was no word on whether Chinese authorities will authorize ZTE's H200 imports, noting China has taken a protectionist stance as it tries to grow its own chip industry, discouraging purchases of foreign technology and pushing firms toward homegrown accelerators such as Huawei's, which the article says has made strides technologically and financially.\n    Quote: Over on the Chinese side of the table, Reuters remarks that there's no word on whether the respective authorities will give ZTE the go-ahead for import, as the country has taken on a protectionist stance as it tries to grow its own chip industry. … The country has discouraged firms from purchasing foreign tech and has instead pushed companies to acquire homegrown accelerators. Huawei in particular\n- e557 [party_statement; CNBC (as reported by Tom's Hardware); original source U.S. trade official (unnamed) 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official, cited by CNBC and speaking at a congressional hearing on 2026-07-14, said that 'very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place' and that it is 'a very small quantity of chips.'\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It’s a very small quantity of chips\" during a congressional hearing.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e490; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is the reported status quo: no public Chinese authorization, which is rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence does not establish that the rung-1 event has already happened: the specific reports describe a plan to let selected firms buy limited quantities, with firms still needing to request approval. But given that named firms were reportedly told they would be permitted to buy, and China was reported to be moving toward individual approvals, at least one public authorization by the January 31, 2027 deadline is more likely than no authorization. Restrictions and the push to reduce foreign-processor purchases weigh against that forecast, but do not outweigh the specific reported plan over the remaining six months. The FY2027 Data Center revenue dependenc\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "ae28d11f3d606f484135adf799377e6b37f20f7c3c01f9cd9a926ea3b7e0a44b": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $700 billion: Combined spending is less than $700 billion. / rung 2 $700 billion to below $800 billion: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion. / rung 3 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 4 $900 billion to below $1000 billion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion. / rung 5 $1000 billion or more: Combined spending is at least $1000 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 9 items]\n- e66 [party_statement; mitrade.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] In its market-wrap section, the item reports that Meta's US$145 billion AI spending plan put chip stocks in focus this week; no period for the spending plan is stated.\n    Quote: Weekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 … Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116.\n- e85 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (2027), according to fool.com, following the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026.\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. … Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e133, e215; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e133 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com (citing NVIDIA management on its last quarterly conference call); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management, on its most recent quarterly conference call, forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026, per this report by Keithen Drury of fool.com.\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e152 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source 247wallst.com ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] 24/7 Wall St. noted that hyperscalers are now having to move into debt and equity offerings and that whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now a central investor question, while it judged demand strong in 2027.\n    Quote: Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds.\n- e215 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] According to the article, Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), versus around $650 billion planned for 2026. This is a forecast attributed to Nvidia, not a reported actual spending figure.\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e224 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source AI hyperscalers (aggregate) ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026 ('this year'), and that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion, which it says will drive higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs. Reported as an aggregate plan/forecast by the author; no individual company guidance or figure is named.\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. … That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e407 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] NVIDIA told investors last quarter that it expects 'AI hyperaccelerator' spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (2027), per the author. This is the company's own forecast of industry spending, not a reported actual figure and not NVIDIA's own revenue.\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\n- e489 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey (247wallst.com); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2027 (the article's 'next year'), adding 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, up from roughly 6.7 GW added in 2025; Google alone is forecast to add 6.8 GW.\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago. … Hyperscalers will spend over $1 trillion on AI next year, with Google alone adding more co\n- e562 [fact; EBC (ebc.com); original source Amazon ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon borrowed another $25 billion, which the article says triggered a minor selloff in AI-related debt amid investor concern about hyperscaler returns on AI capital spending.\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 5. The separate Morgan Stanley forecast also exceeds $1 trillion, though it is for broader AI infrastructure rather than explicitly data-center capex. The 2026 plan of $650 billion followed by a 2027 forecast above $1 trillion implies growth of more than 53.8%; no evidence gives a quantified 2027 reduction. The debt-funding concern and possible 2028 slowdown do not quantify a cut to 2027 spending. The v2 NVIDIA revenue conclusion is compatible with this spending level and does not impose a cap on aggregate hyperscaler capex.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "be0a897c885adca9f717f3bf8cc9498296c1d5759218a92486f6944d879b3348": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v20 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 revenue from Blackwell and Rubin products\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much revenue will NVIDIA report from Blackwell and Rubin products in FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Blackwell and Rubin product revenue is at least $400 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Blackwell and Rubin product revenue is at least $320 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Blackwell and Rubin product revenue is at least $270 billion and below $320 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Blackwell and Rubin product revenue is at least $220 billion and below $270 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Blackwell and Rubin product revenue is below $220 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 45 items]\n[Not shown: 1 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e4 [fact; TradingKey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01] NVIDIA selected Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, a product-architecture decision reported by TradingKey on July 1, 2026; the article describes the DGX Rubin NVL8 as the AI system intended to power data centres over the next two to three years and for the next product cycle, but gives no shipment timing or unit volumes.\n    Quote: NVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. … NVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next \n- e12 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NN, Inc. ; from gnews:9a13ec8bfdc32e585c1f0604 2026-07-01] NN, Inc. (NNBR) announced in late June 2026 a major expansion of its liquid cooling production for NVIDIA AI data center racks, adding 47 new machine centers at its Wuxi, China plant, with 100% of the new capacity already pre-sold and tied to multi-year, multi-product awards. The company said the move deepens its role in NVIDIA's Asia supply chain (while repurposing legacy automotive capacity) and that it targets US$80–US$90 million of accretive new business in 2026.\n    Quote: In late June 2026, NN, Inc. announced a major expansion of its liquid cooling production for NVIDIA AI data center racks, adding 47 new machine centers in its Wuxi, China plant with 100% of this new capacity already pre-sold and tied to multi-year, multi-product awards. … This move effectively deepens NN's role in NVIDIA's Asia supply chain while repurposing legacy automotive capacity and targetin\n- e15 [fact; moomoo.com (citing NVIDIA press release); original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs,\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e26, e29, e34, e38, e64, e81, e90, e114, e154, e266; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e16 [fact; moomoo.com (citing NVIDIA press release); original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus, another first participant in the model, is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e26 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA / Sharon AI ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Sharon AI as one of two initial partners in its revenue-sharing/compute-access program; Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e29 [fact; CNBC (citing Nvidia's announcement); original source Sharon AI 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, an Australian AI cloud provider named as an initial partner in Nvidia's new compute-access program, will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e30 [fact; CNBC (citing Nvidia's announcement); original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies, an AI infrastructure company, is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs, as an initial partner in Nvidia's compute-access program.\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e34 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs and is one of the first companies to adopt Nvidia's revenue-sharing GPU rent-back backstop offer.\n    Quote: Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e38 [fact; Luke James, Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed in an 8-K filing dated June 12 that its agreement with NVIDIA under the new model runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e42 [party_statement; Justin Pope (Motley Fool); original source Jensen Huang / Nvidia ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e75, e326; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e64 [party_statement; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] As an early participant in NVIDIA's new AI-factory compute programme, Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Early participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e75 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in March that he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales cumulatively between that time (March) and the end of 2027. The item gives no split by fiscal year or by product generation.\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e81 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider, plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement with Nvidia. It listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO and raised an additional $1.6 billion in a June 2026 private placement to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory.\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia … The company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e90 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Sharon AI is named as an early adopter of NVIDIA's revenue-sharing financing model and plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under it.\n    Quote: Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e91 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus is named as an early adopter of NVIDIA's revenue-sharing financing model and is developing a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e95 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] According to the report, Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e107 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com (Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis also claimed that Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack configuration after pushback from hyperscaler customers.\n    Quote: The firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e114 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88 billion, an implied floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour on average over the six years, and guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027 as its footprint grows to 132MW, of which 102MW is contracted.\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e121 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\n- e122 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research (the bank) ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\n    Quote: The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\n- e124 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] According to the Morgan Stanley breakdown, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\n- e126 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing procurement structure materially moves final pricing.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\n- e138 [fact; Zacks (via TradingView); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion of revenue in the first quarter of fiscal 2027, 92% of total sales, with segment revenue up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially, which Zacks attributes to the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Quote: The numbers highlight this strength. During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e231, e365, e426, e458, e482; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e154 [fact; Futurum Group; original source Sharon AI / NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Sharon AI is among the first partners under NVIDIA's July 1, 2026 revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangement and is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, according to the Futurum Group's account of the announcement. The figure is a stated deployment plan, not a confirmed order or shipment.\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e174 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com (Cristina Cueto, DCD); original source Bleeding Edge ; from gnews:c2924b0380602940d68de7ff 2026-07-06] Bleeding Edge launched an AI Factory in Querétaro, Mexico, bringing online its neocloud offering, and claims it is the first neocloud in Mexico and Latin America based on Nvidia Blackwell technology. The platform incorporates Nvidia Blackwell B300 systems plus an in-house software layer.\n    Quote: Bleeding Edge has launched an AI Factory in Querétaro, Mexico, bringing online its neocloud offering. … The company claims it is the first neocloud in Mexico and Latin America based on Nvidia Blackwell technology. … The neocloud platform incorporates Nvidia Blackwell B300 systems and a software layer developed in-house for automation, observability, monitoring, and resource management, with the go\n- e176 [party_statement; datacenterdynamics.com (Cristina Cueto, DCD); original source Bleeding Edge ; from gnews:c2924b0380602940d68de7ff 2026-07-06] Bleeding Edge plans to expand both the capacity of its neocloud and its AI Factories in the coming months by incorporating new Nvidia systems and deploying infrastructure in other strategic markets.\n    Quote: Bleeding Edge plans to expand both the capacity of its neocloud and its AI Factories in the coming months by incorporating new Nvidia systems and deploying infrastructure in other strategic markets.\n- e181 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com, citing SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] According to SemiAnalysis, Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall from the scrapped and delayed next-generation rack configurations by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\n- e185 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-06; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted the SemiAnalysis report on Kyber/Rubin Ultra delays, saying: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e131 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported, about a week earlier, that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design due to 'manufacturing execution concerns,' and that the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n- e189 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the article.\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e290 [third_party_view; Investing.com via Yahoo Finance UK; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] BofA analyst Vivek Arya estimated that HBM content per rack may rise by approximately $0.2–0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while rack pricing could increase by $2–3 million, driven by upgrades across compute, networking and software.\n    Quote: While HBM content per rack may rise by approximately $0.2-0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, Arya noted that rack pricing could increase by $2-3 million, driven by upgrades across compute, networking and software.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e301; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e301 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA, in a report summarized by moomoo.com, estimated that HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million in the move from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 million for Vera Rubin — BofA's argument that higher rack pricing offsets HBM inflation.\n    Quote: The key bear case is HBM inflation. BofA does not deny the cost pressure, but argues the market is missing the offsetting pricing power. The report estimates HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 millio\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e290; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e326 [party_statement; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] Nvidia management has articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers, as reported by fool.com's Adam Spatacco.\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e381 [party_statement; Bloomberg (reported by theverge.com); original source SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won ; from gnews:3839887e5ca53a0cb0e7c91e 2026-07-10] SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won said in June 2026 that the company (SK Hynix's parent group, per the article's framing) plans to ramp up its memory chip capacity over the next five years to address a memory shortage that could last until 2030, as reported by Bloomberg. No capacity volumes or amounts are given, and the ramp is a plan, not completed capacity.\n    Quote: SK Group chairman Chey Tae-won said in June that the company plans to ramp up its memory chip capacity over the next five years to address a shortage that could last until 2030, Bloomberg reports .\n- e412 [rumour; tech-insider.org, citing supply-chain sources; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] According to supply-chain reporting summarized in the item, NVIDIA has pulled the RTX 5060 from retail and cut production of existing RTX 50-series cards by an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026.\n    Quote: The RTX 5060 has been pulled from retail, with RTX 50-series production cut an estimated 15% to 20% through at least Q3 2026. … Nvidia has reportedly pulled the RTX 5060 from the market entirely, with supply-chain sources pointing to at least six months before it returns, as part of a broader 15% to 20% production cut across the RTX 50-series through at least Q3 2026.\n- e420 [fact; I/O Fund (io-fund.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2dcfaef1a91c2976a4e347da 2026-07-12] Nvidia has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform, an SSD-based enclosure connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet that uses 64 BlueField-4 DPUs to provide roughly 9,600 TB of SSD capacity per rack, extending effective GPU KV-cache capacity. Nvidia also created the STX reference architecture so storage vendors can build CMX systems that connect to Vera Rubin racks; the I/O Fund notes STX is modular and that operators can add CMX independently of compute, which it calls key to CMX being a new revenue driver for Nvidia.\n    Quote: To solve for KV cache bottlenecks, Nvidia has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform. CMX uses an SSD-based enclosure that is connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet. … CMX incorporates 64 BlueField-4 DPUs, which connect to approximately 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity per rack. … Nvidia also created the STX reference architecture to provide a standardized bluepr\n- e443 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, a reported product launch for which the item gives no shipment volume or timing.\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e256 (); both are in force: Nvidia is working to release an even more powerful line-up of AI processors called Rubin in the second half of 2026, according to The Straits Times.\n- e444 [rumour; en.sedaily.com (Lee Seok-jin), citing the semiconductor industry; original source LG Electronics ; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \"Vera Rubin\" in the first half of the year, according to unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and to begin mass production next year.\n    Quote: LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. The move expands the recent AI infrastructure alliance between LG and Nvidia into server rack manufacturing. … According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototyp\n- e488 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey (247wallst.com); original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed custom ASIC racks costs $6–11 billion, versus roughly $19 billion for Nvidia GB300-based GPU racks; under Nvidia's Vera Rubin generation, rack cost is estimated at about $25 billion per gigawatt. This compares the cost of competitor ASIC-based racks against Nvidia's GPU racks, bearing on Nvidia's competitive cost position and on the economics driving hyperscaler custom-silicon spend.\n    Quote: Broadcom (AVGO) and Marvell (MRVL) design custom AI chips costing between $6 billion and $11 billion per gigawatt, compared to $19 billion for Nvidia GPU racks. … Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera R\n- e496 [party_statement; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027, as cited by the Motley Fool.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\n- e527 [fact; Alex Sirois (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] 247wallst.com reported that NVIDIA's Blackwell 300 is ramping and that the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\n- e545 [party_statement; NVIDIA, as cited by Rest of World; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] NVIDIA said in its earnings report that its newest Blackwell computing architecture line is 'sold out'.\n    Quote: Its newest Blackwell computing architecture line is “sold out,” Nvidia said in the earnings report.\n- e549 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich, cited by Rest of World; original source Kamil Dimmich, North of South Capital ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich, a partner at emerging-markets investment firm North of South Capital, said Humain's first order from Nvidia was for 18,000 of Nvidia's newest Blackwell chips, which he said no rival can yet match for heavy training workloads.\n    Quote: Humain’s first order from Nvidia was 18,000 of its newest Blackwell chips — the hardware that does that heavy lifting, which no rival can yet match, he said.\n- e559 [rumour; EBC (ebc.com), citing reports from SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Over the weekend before the 2026-07-14 article, reports attributed to industry research firm SemiAnalysis circulated suggesting Nvidia's upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028; the report is unconfirmed by Nvidia.\n    Quote: Over the weekend, reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e561 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up in volume and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, according to the article's description of Nvidia's target shipping dates.\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e256; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e570 [fact; jakartaglobe.id; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Under its partnership with Nvidia, Firmus Technologies plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation GPUs in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, to provide AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 5. The evidence still implies spending above $1 trillion: the roughly $650 billion 2026 plan followed by NVIDIA's forecast points to growth of more than 53.8%\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is no change (rung 3): no consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Continued supply tightness through most of 2027 points to a further increase, but the exceptional 2026 price run-up leaves a much higher comparison base; I esti\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the currently reported position—no public Chinese authorization is reported—which sits at rung 2. The specific reports that China planned to permit limited purchases for named firms, followed by Reuters-reported progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s,\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: There is no Blackwell-and-Rubin-specific FY2027 consensus or NVIDIA guidance, so the reference for this question is unplaced. As a broad market proxy, the Data Center reference is about $361 billion (rung 2), but it is not a product-specific estimate. The current dependency estimate of $397–398 billion for Data Center revenue implies about $337–338 billion for Blackwell and Rubin using the carried estimate that they comprise roughly 85% of Data Center revenue; this is below the $400 billion rung-1 boundary and within rung 2. New Rubin shipment timing and higher rack-price estimates support the forecast, but do not quantify enough FY2027 product revenue to lift the implied estimate across that boundary. The estimate remains below the Data Center total, consistent with dependency v2. The upd\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "7ee9faa019afd87de4ad3da526584a627f93977f4f3310383fb04607b4780d38": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: share at least 85%. / rung 2 Bullish: at least 78% and below 85%. / rung 3 Neutral: at least 70% and below 78%. / rung 4 Bearish: at least 60% and below 70%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: below 60%.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 81 items]\n[Not shown: 3 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e32 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Nvidia is acting as a financial backstop for neocloud customers buying its AI chips, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of the customers' cloud revenue, per a company post detailing the new offering. Nvidia said the model lets AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure through 'economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model,' with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of cloud revenue on the supported capacity, and it described this as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream that accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among AI-native customers. Firmus and Sharon AI are named among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer. … \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure f\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e14 (); both are in force: The Information reported that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price; in return NVIDIA will receive a percentage of the providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\n- e45 [party_statement; The Wire China (Noah Berman); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Nvidia presented a report to the U.S. government arguing that Huawei's stockpile of AI chips, together with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner SMIC, give Huawei \"the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand,\" and warning that \"if Nvidia is kept out of the China market, Huawei production and sales will soar.\" The report gave no timeframe and did not address performance differences between Nvidia and Huawei chips.\n    Quote: In a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.” … “If Nvidia is kept out of the China market, Huawei production and sales will soar,”\n- e48 [party_statement; The Wire China; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] In the report presented to the U.S. government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and would sell 1 million in 2025.\n    Quote: In the report it presented to the government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and that it would sell 1 million in 2025.\n- e49 [party_statement; The Wire China; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Nvidia's report estimated that by the end of 2026 SMIC will have made more than 700,000 of Huawei's Ascend 910C chips, reaching production of 50,000 of these chips per month, and that by 2027 SMIC would produce several million of Huawei's less advanced 910B chips.\n    Quote: It also estimated that by the end of this year SMIC — which manufactures chips based on Huawei’s design and technology — will have made more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips, one of Huawei’s most advanced, with production reaching 50,000 of these chips per month. By 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\n- e50 [third_party_view; The Wire China; original source Chris McGuire (Council on Foreign Relations) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Chris McGuire of the Council on Foreign Relations estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025, that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia's chip production that year, and that Nvidia's performance lead will only grow even if Huawei makes 2 million AI chips in 2026 and 4 million in 2027.\n    Quote: In a report published in December, Chris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — a figure at the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year. … McGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, \n- e51 [third_party_view; The Wire China; original source Epoch AI ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] According to research institute Epoch AI, Huawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide in the first quarter of 2026, and about half that amount after adjusting for the fact that its chips are less powerful than Nvidia's.\n    Quote: Huawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year, and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that the company’s chips are not as powerful as Nvidia’s, according to Epoch AI, a research institute.\n- e52 [fact; The Wire China; original source Huawei ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Huawei introduced a new chip, the 950PR, in March 2026 and is preparing to launch another later in 2026; according to Lennart Heim, these chips fail to match the H200 because they have slower memory and lag even further behind Nvidia's Blackwell chips.\n    Quote: Huawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year. Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\n- e53 [fact; The Wire China; original source Reuters (as cited by NVIDIA) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Nvidia's report cited Reuters reporting that Huawei had built a stockpile of between 2 and 3 million of its own AI chips made by TSMC, which Huawei obtained via illegal diversion by the Chinese company Sophgo.\n    Quote: The report also cited Reuters reporting that Huawei had built a stockpile of between 2 and 3 million of its own AI chips made by Taiwanese chipmaker TSMC, which it had obtained via illegal diversion by the Chinese company Sophgo.\n- e55 [third_party_view; The Wire China; original source Lennart Heim ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Lennart Heim said figures such as 2 to 3 million AI chips are tiny relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of 2026.\n    Quote: Even though figures such as 2 to 3 million AI chips sound significant, “this number is tiny” relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips that the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of this year, Heim adds.\n- e58 [party_statement; The Diplomat (Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander); original source Jensen Huang / NVIDIA ; from gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang estimated that for every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia's GPUs, implying Nvidia captures a roughly 60% share of U.S. AI company spending. The estimate is undated and refers to U.S. AI companies' spending rather than total global data-center accelerator spending.\n    Quote: For every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia’s GPUs, Huang estimated .\n- e77 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com; original source Cerebras (and Space Exploration Technologies Corp) ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] fool.com reported that Nvidia faces competitors including Cerebras and Space Exploration Technologies Corp, which have promised more powerful GPUs than Nvidia, and that Cerebras says it already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\n    Quote: There are competitors like Cerebras and Space Exploration Technologies Corp , which have promised to make more powerful GPUs than Nvidia, and Cerebras already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\n- e144 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source 247wallst.com ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] 24/7 Wall St. stated that NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market.\n    Quote: NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market\n- e146 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Broadcom guided third-quarter AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year, per 24/7 Wall St.\n    Quote: Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e233, e435, e466, e534; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e147 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source AMD ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] AMD has a Meta Instinct GPU deal, and per CEO Lisa Su, leading customer forecasts for its MI450 exceed the company's initial expectations, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\n    Quote: AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e232, e469; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e162 [third_party_view; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Omor Ibne Ehsan, 24/7 Wall St. 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] 24/7 Wall St. estimates that about 50% of NVIDIA's data center revenue comes from hyperscalers, the same companies funding Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta