erified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e147; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e241 [rumour; Reuters; original source DeepSeek 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek is developing its own custom AI chip for inference rather than training, a project Reuters reported (citing three unnamed people) has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with the company quietly recruiting chip engineers and in talks with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers (no partners named). No tape-out or production timeline has been disclosed; the article says a finished chip is likely years away and that U.S. export controls on advanced foundries and HBM are a structural bottleneck.\n    Quote: DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1] … The project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2] … The project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with D\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e247, e322; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e242 [fact; mlq.ai; original source DeepSeek ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round, its first outside capital raise, at a valuation above $50 billion, with backing including Tencent, CATL and founder Liang Wenfeng, who personally contributed roughly $2.9 billion; the article says the round's limited-partnership structure with a five-year lock-up for most investors, and the resulting war chest, give DeepSeek capital to sustain a multi-year hardware development effort.\n    Quote: DeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4] … Founder Liang Wenfeng personally contributed roughly $2.9 billion to the round, and the deal's unusual structure — a limited partnership with a five-year lock-up for most investors — suggests the company is planning on a long horizon [4] . … DeepSeek's $7.5 billion w\n- e250 [fact; idnfinancials.com; original source OpenAI (with Broadcom); Anthropic (reported consideration) ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] As part of a broader trend of major AI companies designing their own chips, OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, in collaboration with Broadcom; Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\n    Quote: Globally, DeepSeek's move follows a broader trend among major AI companies developing their own chips. OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom, while Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\n- e263 [fact; The Information (cited by The Next Web); original source Nvidia and d-Matrix ; from gnews:c2ccca1a61b99adc685b1aa2 2026-07-08] Nvidia is combining its own hardware with inference chips from chip startup d-Matrix, and the two companies will ship a joint system to run AI models, with AI-cloud firm Parasail as the first customer and the system due to come online later this year, according to a report by The Information cited by The Next Web.\n    Quote: The GPU giant is combining its hardware with inference chips from the startup d-Matrix , The Information reported. The two will ship a joint system to run AI models. The AI-cloud firm Parasail will be its first customer, with the system due online later this year.\n- e264 [party_statement; The Next Web; original source d-Matrix (CEO Sid Sheth) ; from gnews:c2ccca1a61b99adc685b1aa2 2026-07-08] d-Matrix CEO Sid Sheth says the startup's Corsair inference accelerator, built on in-memory computing that puts compute and memory on one chip, skips the pricey, scarce high-bandwidth memory that Nvidia's chips rely on, and d-Matrix claims that pairing Corsair with GPUs runs tokens about 10 times faster at roughly a third of the cost and up to five times less energy on some jobs. The item notes these are the company's own figures, not independent results.\n    Quote: d-Matrix built its Corsair accelerator around “in-memory computing”, which keeps data next to the logic that crunches it. … CEO Sid Sheth says its edge is to put compute and memory on one chip. It also skips the pricey, scarce high-bandwidth memory that Nvidia’s chips lean on. The startup claims that pairing Corsair with GPUs runs tokens about 10 times faster , at roughly a third of the cost and u\n- e271 [fact; CNBC; original source JPMorgan Chase 2026-07-08; from gnews:6b89157155648b9ec317d209 2026-07-08] JPMorgan Chase selected SambaNova as its inference provider and said it would deploy SambaNova's systems for on-prem inference in its enterprise AI workloads, according to an announcement reported on Wednesday (2026-07-08); SambaNova CEO Rodrigo Liang confirmed the selection. This is a named large bank choosing a rival AI-inference chip provider rather than incumbent GPU vendor NVIDIA.\n    Quote: On Wednesday, JPMorgan Chase said it would deploy SambaNova's systems for \"on-prem inference in our demanding enterprise AI workloads.\" … \"JPMorgan has selected SambaNova to be the inference provider for the bank,\" Liang said.\n- e280 [third_party_view; tradingview.com (GuruFocus article); original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:a09988fd9c5c1a739a14f873 2026-07-08] According to Bloomberg Intelligence data cited in the article, NVIDIA held 97% of the server GPU market at the end of 2025, up from 95% at the end of 2024.\n    Quote: Nvidia's market position still appears strong, with the company holding 97% of the server GPU market at the end of 2025, up from 95% at the end of 2024, according to Bloomberg Intelligence data cited in the source.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e357, e423; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e281 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Fortune); original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released on Tuesday, found that respondents will allocate 46% of their AI accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today.\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e283 [third_party_view; Bloomberg (Fortune); original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey concluded that NVIDIA, while still popular in China, is expected to see its market share shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local firms take over.\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e205; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e292 [party_statement; theregister.com; original source SambaNova ; from gnews:b2ff1fae25f82fd3111134de 2026-07-08] SambaNova said this week that it shared benchmark results, conducted by the independent testing firm Artificial Analysis, for its SN50-series inference platform, which pairs Nvidia GPUs with SambaNova's own accelerators. Artificial Analysis found the heterogeneous platform — four Nvidia H200 GPUs handling the compute-intensive prefill phase plus a single SambaNova rack of 16 SN50 Reconfigurable Dataflow Units handling memory-bandwidth-bound decode — reached a decode speed of 763 tokens per second on MiniMax M2.7 at short context lengths (10,000 input tokens), which the item describes as several times faster than competing inference providers running on GPUs alone. SambaNova separately says its platform sustains more than 450 tokens per second at longer context lengths.\n    Quote: This week, the AI chip startup shared benchmark results showing its latest generation of AI acceleration, which combines Nvidia GPUs and the company's accelerators, beating GPU-only inference platforms by a wide margin. … The testing, conducted by the AI benchmarking gurus at Artificial Analysis, showed SambaNova's SN50-series accelerators, announced in February, churning out 763 tokens a second i\n- e293 [party_statement; theregister.com; original source SambaNova ; from gnews:b2ff1fae25f82fd3111134de 2026-07-08] SambaNova said its systems are air-cooled and can be deployed in existing datacenters, a contrast it drew with Nvidia's latest-generation Rubin GPUs, which the item says absolutely need liquid cooling.\n    Quote: And because its systems are air-cooled, they can be deployed in existing datacenters — something that can't be said of Nvidia's latest generation of Rubin GPUs , which absolutely need liquid cooling.\n- e303 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA (Bank of America) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA's report says Nvidia's hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, which BofA reads as Nvidia gaining wallet share rather than losing share to custom chips.\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia’s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\n    Opinion, not verified: the 