n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e280; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e364 [fact; Reuters, as reported by gurufocus.com; original source Meta Platforms (plan reported by Reuters) ; from gnews:09b9d76297a57f198651c5e7 2026-07-10] Reuters reported that Meta Platforms plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom and manufactured by TSMC, and the report said it is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics processors Meta continues to buy from Nvidia and Advanced Micro Devices.\n    Quote: Reuters said Meta plans to begin manufacturing its custom AI processor, code-named Iris, in September after completing initial testing. The chip is being designed with Broadcom (AVGO) and manufactured by Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSM). … The report said the processor is intended to support Meta's AI infrastructure while complementing, rather than replacing, the large number of graphics p\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e349; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e372 [rumour; gurufocus.com; original source  ; from gnews:4c201dc59ba0c7579bfef4db 2026-07-10] GuruFocus reported rumours that major NVIDIA clients, including Meta, are accelerating development of their own in-house AI chips, a shift the article said could pressure NVIDIA's share of the AI market. The rumour is attributed to unnamed sources, gives no figure, date or confirmed chip programme detail, and is accompanied by the view that NVIDIA's share within Meta may fall.\n    Quote: On July 10, 2026, NVIDIA's NVDA stock experienced a notable increase, rising 4% to $210.96, despite rumors of major clients accelerating their development of in-house artificial intelligence (AI) chips. … NVIDIA's stock surge comes amidst growing concerns that major clients, such as Meta, are shifting towards developing their own AI chips. This strategic pivot could potentially impact NVIDIA's mar\n- e401 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Silicon Analysts, compiling estimates from TrendForce, Morgan Stanley and TSMC capacity data, put Nvidia's share of AI training chips above 90% in 2025, with its inference share between 60% and 75% over the same period, a gap of roughly 15 to 30 percentage points that it says challengers are targeting.\n    Quote: According to Silicon Analysts , which compiles estimates from TrendForce, Morgan Stanley $MS , and TSMC $TSM capacity data, Nvidia 's share exceeded 90% in training in 2025. Its inference share sat between 60% and 75% over the same period, undercut by growing competition from custom silicon. Between the two ranges, the gap works out to roughly 15 to 30 percentage points.\n- e402 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Mizuho Securities and Silicon Analysts ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Mizuho Securities estimated Nvidia's overall AI chip market share at between 70% and 95% depending on segment, and Silicon Analysts projected that share would settle near 75% as the total AI chip market expands past $200 billion.\n    Quote: Nvidia 's overall AI chip market share, estimated by Mizuho Securities at between 70% and 95% depending on segment, is projected by Silicon Analysts to settle near 75% as the total market expands past $200 billion.\n- e403 [fact; qz.com, citing TechCrunch; original source Amazon (AWS) ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] As of March, Amazon had 1.4 million Trainium chips deployed across three generations, according to TechCrunch, with Anthropic's Claude running on more than one million Trainium2 chips alone; AWS offers Trainium to external customers through its EC2 cloud rental service.\n    Quote: Amazon $AMZN is the closest any cloud provider has come to building a real open-market rival to Nvidia . AWS, Amazon 's cloud division, has made its Trainium chips available to external customers through cloud rental service EC2. As of March, 1.4 million Trainium chips were deployed across three generations, according to TechCrunch , with Anthropic's Claude running on more than one million Trainiu\n- e404 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon (AWS) ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Amazon has committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI, placing its custom AI chips inside one of the industry's most prominent AI labs.\n    Quote: Amazon has also committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI, putting its chips inside two of the industry's most prominent AI labs.\n- e408 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] Broadcom is making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic and OpenAI, in addition to Alphabet's Tensor Processing Units, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year (2026) and into next year; Broadcom expects major growth next year as new clients launch their custom AI chips. The item states this as the backdrop to custom AI silicon competing with GPU-based computing from Nvidia and AMD.\n    Quote: Broadcom expects major growth next year as new clients launch their custom AI chips. Currently, Broadcom's major customer is Alphabet , with its Tensor Processing Units (TPUs). However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\n- e421 [fact; I/O Fund (io-fund.com); original source Google ; from gnews:2dcfaef1a91c2976a4e347da 2026-07-12] Google's inference-optimized TPU 8i pods scale to 1,152 TPUs with pod-level HBM capacity of 331.8TB shared across all 1,152 chips over Google's inter-chip interconnect (ICI), which