ifferent, more concentrated way to profit from the same build-out.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e146; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e451 [rumour; The Information, via GuruFocus; original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google (Alphabet) has approached 'neocloud' companies — specialized cloud providers that rent computing power to AI developers and businesses — about using its tensor processing units (TPUs), according to a report from The Information, an effort that puts Google in more direct competition with Nvidia.\n    Quote: Google has approached so-called neocloud companies about using its tensor processing units, or TPUs, according to a report from The Information. These specialized cloud providers rent computing power to AI developers and businesses.\n- e452 [rumour; The Information, via GuruFocus; original source Nscale ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Nscale, a neocloud provider, reportedly told Google that its active projects remain centered on GPU capacity, indicating that at least one specialized cloud provider is staying on Nvidia GPUs rather than adopting Google's TPUs.\n    Quote: One provider, Nscale, reportedly told Google that its active projects remain centered on GPU capacity.\n- e453 [fact; GuruFocus; original source Google (Alphabet) and Blackstone ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google is working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms next year (2027).\n    Quote: Google is also working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms next year.\n- e464 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (Alex Sirois); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] Broadcom reported Q2 FY2026 revenue of $22.187 billion, up 47.9%, including AI semiconductor sales of $10.80 billion growing 143%; C