sing Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera R\n- e503 [third_party_view; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Analysts (unnamed, as cited by the article) ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] According to the article, analysts estimate NVIDIA holds about an 80% share of the AI data center chip market and anticipate its AI chip market share will decline to 75% this year (2026), reflecting competition from AMD and Broadcom and customers designing in-house chips.\n    Quote: So, there is still a lot of room for Nvidia to boost its data center chip revenue over the next five years, especially considering that it is the dominant player in this market with an estimated 80% share. … However, analysts believe that Nvidia's AI data center chip market share may have peaked. That's not surprising, as competitors Advanced Micro Devices and Broadcom have been making solid strid\n- e509 [fact; David Jagielski, The Motley Fool; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:cf6b0ccecbe4028542c4e178 2026-07-14] Broadcom (a maker of custom AI accelerators competing with NVIDIA) reported a growth rate of 48% in its most recent quarter, which ended May 3, and said its semiconductor revenue attributable to AI rose by 143%, coming in above its own forecast.\n    Quote: Its growth rate was 48% in its most recent quarter (which ended May 3). And the company says that semiconductor revenue due to AI rose by 143%, coming in even higher than its forecast.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e464; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e510 [fact; David Jagielski, The Motley Fool; original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:cf6b0ccecbe4028542c4e178 2026-07-14] Advanced Micro Devices, NVIDIA's main GPU rival, reported revenue of $10.3 billion for the period ending March 28, up 38% year over year; the article frames AMD's newest chips as an attempt to become viable alternatives for NVIDIA customers.\n    Quote: For the period ending March 28, the company's revenue rose by 38% to $10.3 billion. … The company hopes that its newest chips become viable alternatives for Nvidia customers to consider.\n- e519 [third_party_view; Harsh Chauhan (fool.com); original source fool.com 2026-07-14; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] fool.com (author Harsh Chauhan) reports that Marvell Technology is one of the key players in custom AI processors, controlling 20% to 25% of that market, with the article arguing such specialized custom chips perform designated tasks faster and with higher power efficiency than GPUs.\n    Quote: The specialized nature of these custom processors means they can perform their designated tasks faster than GPUs and with higher power efficiency. Marvell is one of the key players in custom AI processors, controlling 20% to 25% of this market.\n- e520 [party_statement; Harsh Chauhan (fool.com); original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell Technology claims it designs custom AI chips for the top four U.S. hyperscalers and says it is also expanding its relationships with emerging hyperscalers; the article adds that the company designs custom AI chips for Amazon, Microsoft and others.\n    Quote: The company designs custom AI chips for Amazon , Microsoft , and others. Marvell claims it designs chips for the top four U.S. hyperscalers and is also expanding its relationships with emerging hyperscalers.\n- e521 [party_statement; Harsh Chauhan (fool.com); original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell Technology states it can address more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers over the long run, implying potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\n- e534 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom (Hock Tan) ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan, quoted in the article, said 'the momentum continues' and that Broadcom's Q3 AI semiconductor revenue is set to grow more than 200% year over year to $16 billion.\n    Quote: Broadcom’s Hock Tan says “the momentum continues” with Q3 AI semi revenue set to grow over 200% year-over-year to $16 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e146; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e535 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] Broadcom's AI semiconductor revenue grew 143% year over year to $10.8 billion in its most recent quarter, according to the article.\n    Quote: AI semi revenue grew 143% YoY to $10.8B last quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e464; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e537 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Advanced Micro Devices (AMD) ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] AMD reported Q1 FY2026 revenue of $10.25 billion, up 37.9% year over year, with Data Center segment revenue of $5.78 billion, up 57%, according to the article.\n    Quote: AMD Q1 FY26 revenue was $10.25B (+37.9%) with Data Center at $5.78B (+57%).\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e468; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e546 [fact; Rest of World (Indranil Ghosh); original source G42 (UAE) ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] G42, the UAE's state-backed AI firm, is building the Stargate data center in Abu Dhabi; its first stage will run on 400,000 Nvidia chips.\n    Quote: The UAE is investing on the same scale through its state-backed AI firm G42, which is building a data center in Abu Dhabi called Stargate. Its first stage will run on 400,000 Nvidia chips .\n- e547 [party_statement; Peng Xiao, G42 CEO, in a January interview cited by Rest of World; original source G42 (Peng Xiao, CEO) ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] G42 CEO Peng Xiao said in a January interview that although G42 had spent months trying to spread its orders among several suppliers, the machines inside its Stargate data center would be 'mostly' Nvidia.\n    Quote: G42 has spent months trying to spread its orders among several suppliers, yet the machines inside Stargate would be “mostly” Nvidia, CEO Peng Xiao said in an interview in January.\n- e548 [fact; Rest of World (Indranil Ghosh); original source Humain (Saudi Arabia, PIF-backed) ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Humain, the venture set up by Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund, is building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam powered by several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\n    Quote: Humain is also building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam, powered by several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\n- e550 [fact; Rest of World (Indranil Ghosh); original source Humain (Saudi Arabia, PIF-backed) ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Humain struck a $10 billion deal with AMD for 500 megawatts of computing power, a $2 billion agreement with Groq to run its chatbot, and a tie-up with Qualcomm to fill a Saudi data center with 200 megawatts of its chips, as part of an effort to widen its supplier list beyond Nvidia.\n    Quote: Humain has tried hard to widen its list of suppliers. Last August, it struck a $10 billion deal with AMD for 500 megawatts of computing power, a $2 billion agreement with Groq to run its chatbot, and a tie-up with Qualcomm to fill a Saudi data center with 200 megawatts of its chips.\n- e551 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich, cited by Rest of World; original source Kamil Dimmich, North of South Capital ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich of North of South Capital said the AMD, Qualcomm and Groq chips Humain bought serve a narrower set of tasks than Nvidia's, running finished AI models cheaply for inference while training a powerful model still calls for Nvidia chips; he added that cheaper ASIC designs for inference still need foundry capacity at TSMC.\n    Quote: The deals give the Gulf chips that can run AI, but for a narrower set of tasks than those from Nvidia. The Qualcomm and Groq chips are built to run finished AI models cheaply, for everyday tasks like answering a user’s questions, while training a powerful model still calls for Nvidia’s chips, Kamil Dimmich, a partner at emerging-markets investment firm North of South Capital, told Rest of World . \n- e552 [third_party_view; Sam Winter-Levy, cited by Rest of World; original source Sam Winter-Levy, Carnegie Endowment for International Peace ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Sam Winter-Levy, a fellow at the Carnegie Endowment for International Peace, said the U.S. has told the Gulf that access to its top chips depends on shutting Chinese equipment out, and that chips from AMD or Qualcomm carry the same requirement of Washington's approval because they are U.S. products.\n    Quote: In addition, the U.S. has told the Gulf that access to its top chips depends on shutting Chinese equipment out, Winter-Levy said. … Chips from AMD or Qualcomm come with the same strings attached as those from Nvidia, because all of them are U.S. products that need Washington’s approval before they can be sold to the Gulf, Winter-Levy said.\n- e555 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government / Kingsoft Cloud subsidiary ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] According to the article, an apparent subsidiary of Kingsoft Cloud received U.S. approval to buy AMD AI accelerators equivalent to Nvidia's H200 (presumably Instinct MI300X-class chips).\n    Quote: Additionally, an apparent subsidiary of Kingsoft Cloud got approval to buy AMD accelerators equivalent to Nvidia's H200, presumably Instinct MI300X-class chips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e540; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e565 [fact; mlq.ai; original source FBI ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] The FBI's RFI makes Google TPU v8/v7L AI pods (or better), with performance matching five Nvidia GB300 NVL72 racks, one of its four hardware categories, and requires the TPU option to support multiple AI frameworks; the item notes Google has historically kept TPUs inside its own data centers and offered access only through Google Cloud, making on-premises TPU deployments outside Google's infrastructure extremely rare, and that the FBI may split procurement between Nvidia hardware and TPU pods.\n    Quote: Category 3 is the most ambitious: an AI pod architecture equivalent to Google TPU v8 or v7L, or better, with performance matching five GB300 NVL72 systems. The RFI requires support for multiple AI frameworks, suggesting the FBI wants vendor-neutral software flexibility even on Google's custom silicon [1] . … The inclusion of Google TPUs in Category 3 is the most unusual element of the RFI. Google \n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 5. The evidence still implies spending above $1 trillion: the roughly $650 billion 2026 plan followed by NVIDIA's forecast points to growth of more than 53.8%\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is no change (rung 3): no consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Continued supply tightness through most of 2027 points to a further increase, but the exceptional 2026 price run-up leaves a much higher comparison base; I esti\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the currently reported position—no public Chinese authorization is reported—which sits at rung 2. The specific reports that China planned to permit limited purchases for named firms, followed by Reuters-reported progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s,\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The closest broad-market reference is around 75% (rung 3): the estimate for a 70%–75% share through 2030 and the projection near 75% are more comparable than the 97% server-GPU figure or the roughly 81% figure for a differently defined AI-chip market. I lower the forecast modestly from that reference because custom silicon is no longer only a long-dated possibility: Trainium is deployed at scale, other hyperscalers have shipping or deploying alternatives, and Broadcom reports rapidly growing AI-chip revenue with a $16 billion next-quarter guide. Against that, NVIDIA's data-center sales are growing strongly, and several rival programmes remain planned or serve narrower workloads. The resulting estimate remains within rung 3's 70%–below-78% band; the evidence does not support a move below 70\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "7b4ddb7434916d4660ab16f3b4624f39b19fa022846becc578a4b57eaa16b8b3": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v7 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether Nvidia's Rubin generation begins shipping by year-end 2026\", 2026, due 2026-12-31): Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026. / rung 2 No: Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 22 items]\n- e4 [fact; TradingKey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01] NVIDIA selected Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, a product-architecture decision reported by TradingKey on July 1, 2026; the article describes the DGX Rubin NVL8 as the AI system intended to power data centres over the next two to three years and for the next product cycle, but gives no shipment timing or unit volumes.\n    Quote: NVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. … NVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next \n- e41 [party_statement; Justin Pope (Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is reported to be in full production and could begin shipping later this year (2026).\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e95, e189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e83 [party_statement; bgr.com (Jonathan Sayers); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] In a blog post by Josh Parker, Nvidia's head of corporate sustainability, Nvidia announced that its DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles as part of a 100% liquid-cooled design. The item does not quantify Rubin shipments or revenue, but indicates Rubin infrastructure is being deployed in Nvidia's liquid-cooled data-center design.\n    Quote: In Nvidia's blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at Nvidia, it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles.\n- e95 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] According to the report, Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e100 [party_statement; Fool.com (Adria Cimino); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] According to the article, Nvidia is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall (2026) as part of its Vera Rubin platform.\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\n- e124 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] According to the Morgan Stanley breakdown, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\n- e143 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source  ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] 24/7 Wall St. reported NVIDIA shares were under pressure on reports of delays across the upcoming lineup of its Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are attributed to unnamed sources.\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\n- e185 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, via Investing.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-06; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson, in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, refuted the SemiAnalysis report on Kyber/Rubin Ultra delays, saying: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e131 (); both are in force: SemiAnalysis separately reported, about a week earlier, that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design due to 'manufacturing execution concerns,' and that the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n- e189 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and are set to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to the article.\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e199 [third_party_view; Luke James, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] The article stated that the reported Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\n    Quote: The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\n- e200 [party_statement; Nvidia representative, via Tom's Hardware; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An Nvidia representative responded to Tom's Hardware's questions about the reported Kyber delay with the statement \"Our roadmap is intact,\" providing no further details; the article noted it is unclear whether this refers to the original roadmap and delivery schedules or to a revised roadmap.\n    Quote: An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \"Our roadmap is intact.\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions. As such, it is unclear if Nvidia's statement refers to its original roadmap, planned delivery schedules, and hardware, or if those particulars had already been changed and the statement refers to a newly updated roadmap.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e256 [party_statement; The Straits Times; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] Nvidia is working to release an even more powerful line-up of AI processors called Rubin in the second half of 2026, according to The Straits Times.\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e561; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e270 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, reported by Futuriom; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson said, in response to the Kyber setback report, that \"Our road map is intact\" and that \"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\"\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e335; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e143 (); both are in force: 24/7 Wall St. reported NVIDIA shares were under pressure on reports of delays across the upcoming lineup of its Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are attributed to unnamed sources.\n- e324 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e 2026-07-09] Nvidia denied the speculation that its Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track.\n    Quote: However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e199 (); both are in force: The article stated that the reported Kyber delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\n- e352 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay and confirming that its product roadmap remains intact, a clarification the item frames as countering concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt its AI competitive lead.\n    Quote: NVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e399 [rumour; Jim Cramer, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Semi Analysis (and unnamed reports) ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] Reports indicated a delay to NVIDIA's next-generation AI chips. Per Jim Cramer, the research firm Semi Analysis said 'everything could be late for NVIDIA because one particular part of their business is late,' referring to the Kyber platform. The reports are unconfirmed and Cramer noted he could not prove the story.\n    Quote: AI giant NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is currently in the news these days due to reports of a delay to its next generation AI chips. … this Kyber delay, what's changed, what it means, what happened. You know Semi Analysis does pretty good work, saying that everything could be late for NVIDIA because one particular part of their business is late.\n- e420 [fact; I/O Fund (io-fund.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2dcfaef1a91c2976a4e347da 2026-07-12] Nvidia has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform, an SSD-based enclosure connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet that uses 64 BlueField-4 DPUs to provide roughly 9,600 TB of SSD capacity per rack, extending effective GPU KV-cache capacity. Nvidia also created the STX reference architecture so storage vendors can build CMX systems that connect to Vera Rubin racks; the I/O Fund notes STX is modular and that operators can add CMX independently of compute, which it calls key to CMX being a new revenue driver for Nvidia.\n    Quote: To solve for KV cache bottlenecks, Nvidia has developed its CMX Context Memory Storage Platform. CMX uses an SSD-based enclosure that is connected to Rubin GPUs and Vera CPUs over Spectrum-X Ethernet. … CMX incorporates 64 BlueField-4 DPUs, which connect to approximately 9,600 TB of SSD storage capacity per rack. … Nvidia also created the STX reference architecture to provide a standardized bluepr\n- e443 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, a reported product launch for which the item gives no shipment volume or timing.\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e256 (); both are in force: Nvidia is working to release an even more powerful line-up of AI processors called Rubin in the second half of 2026, according to The Straits Times.\n- e444 [rumour; en.sedaily.com (Lee Seok-jin), citing the semiconductor industry; original source LG Electronics ; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics' Production Research Institute completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \"Vera Rubin\" in the first half of the year, according to unnamed semiconductor-industry sources; LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and to begin mass production next year.\n    Quote: LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. The move expands the recent AI infrastructure alliance between LG and Nvidia into server rack manufacturing. … According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototyp\n- e527 [fact; Alex Sirois (247wallst.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] 247wallst.com reported that NVIDIA's Blackwell 300 is ramping and that the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\n- e560 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, via EBC (ebc.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] An Nvidia spokesperson denied the SemiAnalysis delay report, stating that the company's \"roadmap is intact\".\n    Quote: Although an Nvidia spokesperson quickly countered the reports, telling that the company's \"roadmap is intact,\" the seeds of doubt were already sown.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e561 [party_statement; EBC (ebc.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Nvidia's Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up in volume and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, according to the article's description of Nvidia's target shipping dates.\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e256; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 5. The evidence still implies spending above $1 trillion: the roughly $650 billion 2026 plan followed by NVIDIA's forecast points to growth of more than 53.8%\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is no change (rung 3): no consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Continued supply tightness through most of 2027 points to a further increase, but the exceptional 2026 price run-up leaves a much higher comparison base; I esti\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the currently reported position—no public Chinese authorization is reported—which sits at rung 2. The specific reports that China planned to permit limited purchases for named firms, followed by Reuters-reported progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s,\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is Nvidia’s stated second-half 2026 release plan, reinforced by the reported fall shipment schedule; that expectation sits at rung 1 (Yes). The evidence still implies initial shipments in Q3 or fall 2026, with a Q4 volume ramp. Reports of full production and shipments to eight cloud partners, plus Morgan Stanley’s specific Q3 schedule, outweigh the unconfirmed delay reports: the more specific account scopes the delay to Rubin Ultra/Kyber, not the 2026 Rubin GPUs. The reported Rubin platform launch and related infrastructure add evidence of progress, while LG’s plan to mass-produce a matching rack next year is a caveat but does not establish a constraint on Nvidia’s initial shipments. No evidence establishes that shipments have already begun, but the most likely outcome by the\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "850f7ba5425627dfb0634c12e2227974f201c1587fefab32062cb12a913307e9": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v17 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending in calendar 2026\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What share of China's calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 30%. / rung 2 Bullish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 15% and below 30%. / rung 3 Neutral: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 5% and below 15%. / rung 4 Bearish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 1% and below 5%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is below 1%.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 64 items]\n[Not shown: 1 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e36 [fact; BBC; original source US government ; from gnews:a6ed98dfdf1ad76f6edffdfd 2026-07-02] The BBC reports that the US has since approved the sale of some of Nvidia's semiconductors to China, under certain conditions.\n    Quote: The US has since approved the sale of some of Nvidia's semiconductors to China, under certain conditions .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46, e491; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e46 [fact; The Wire China; original source Trump administration ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December, after the U.S. government had previously restricted such sales under Biden-era export controls on the chip (launched in 2023).\n    Quote: President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e47 [party_statement; The Wire China; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has said that, since the Biden administration first imposed AI-chip export controls in 2022, Nvidia's market share in China has fallen to zero.\n    Quote: Since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\n- e54 [fact; The Wire China; original source Chinese government ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for H200 exports.\n    Quote: The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255, e330, e332; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e67 [fact; The Diplomat (Sebastian Strangio); original source Singapore Police Force 2026-07-01; from gnews:1eeceeb212a2c82c2e75e806 2026-07-02] Singaporean authorities said yesterday (reported by The Diplomat on 2026-07-02) that they seized a luxury bungalow valued at about S$55 million (US$42.4 million) under a 'prohibition of disposal order' and seized around S$1 million of funds, and charged four people with money laundering and fraud as part of an investigation linked to the alleged movement of Nvidia chips subject to U.S. export controls.\n    Quote: Singaporean authorities said yesterday that they have seized a luxury bungalow and charged four people with money laundering as part of an investigation linked to the alleged movement of Nvidia chips subject to U.S. export controls. … In a statement , the Singapore Police Force (SPF) said that it had issued a “prohibition of disposal order” against a residence valued at around S$55 million ($42.4 \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e35; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e68 [fact; The Diplomat (Sebastian Strangio); original source Singapore Police Force 2026-07-01; from gnews:1eeceeb212a2c82c2e75e806 2026-07-02] Singapore police charged Aperia Group officers with conspiring to commit fraud in the purchase of servers from U.S. companies Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus, alleging that between November 2023 and February 2025 they falsely represented to the suppliers that an Aperia Group company would be the end-user of the servers (which may have contained advanced Nvidia AI chips).\n    Quote: The fraud charges allege that the three “engaged in a conspiracy to commit fraud in respect of the purchase of servers” from the U.S. companies Dell, Super Micro Computer, and Asus. The charges assert that between November 2023 and February 2025, the trio “falsely represented, in communications to these suppliers, that one of the Aperia Group companies would be the end-user of the servers.”\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104, e237; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e69 [fact; The Diplomat (Sebastian Strangio); original source Malaysian authorities ; from gnews:1eeceeb212a2c82c2e75e806 2026-07-02] As reported on 2026-07-02, Malaysian authorities said last week that they had foiled an attempt to smuggle advanced AI chips through the country's main airport, seizing 72 servers worth nearly $13 million.\n    Quote: Last week, Malaysian authorities said they had foiled an attempt to smuggle advanced AI chips through the country’s main airport, seizing 72 servers worth nearly $13 million.\n- e70 [fact; CommonWealth Magazine; original source Singapore authorities 2026-07-01; from gnews:3345f0d91321165a099860c9 2026-07-02] Singapore authorities issued a prohibition of disposal order against a luxury home valued at about SGD 55 million ($42 million), freezing the asset and preventing its purchase or sale, and seized about SGD 1 million in bank accounts under investigation, as part of a case of suspected cross-border smuggling of hardware containing Nvidia chips. Lim Jenny and Woon Guo Jie Aaron, two people suspected of involvement in the alleged smuggling, face money laundering charges, and three individuals were brought to court on July 1 and charged with various offences including fraud and money laundering.\n    Quote: Singapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected cross-border smuggling of hardware containing Nvidia chips. … A prohibition of disposal order was issued against the luxury home, valued at about SGD 55M ($42M), freezing the asset and preventing its purchase or sale. Funds in bank accounts under investigation, va\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e35; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e71 [fact; CommonWealth Magazine; original source Singapore authorities ; from gnews:3345f0d91321165a099860c9 2026-07-02] Four companies — Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech and Aperia Cloud Services (II), collectively the Aperia group, plus Luxuriate Your Life — are facing charges in Singapore for false representation over the alleged export of computer servers that might contain Nvidia chips to Malaysia. Luxuriate Your Life was charged on July 1, while the other three firms will be charged on July 6.\n    Quote: Four firms allegedly linked to the export of computer servers that might contain Nvidia chips to Malaysia are facing charges for false representation. … The companies are Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech, and Aperia Cloud Services (II) — collectively known as the Aperia group — and Luxuriate Your Life. … Luxuriate Your Life was charged on July 1, while the other three firms will be charged o\n- e163 [party_statement; Omor Ibne Ehsan, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA (Colette Kress, CFO) ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said China data center compute revenue is effectively zero and that losing the market, which NVIDIA sizes at close to about $50 billion in the future, would be material, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\n    Quote: China data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material.\n- e193 [fact; The Straits Times; original source Singapore Police Force / Singapore courts (charges against Alan Wei Zhaolun and three Aperia-linked companies) 2026-07-06; from gnews:f0d529209a91c7a237e117f1 2026-07-06] On 6 July 2026, Singapore authorities handed Alan Wei Zhaolun, 50, the Singaporean former chief executive of A-Speed Infotech, Aperia International and Aperia Cloud Services (II), nine additional charges – three counts of money laundering and six counts of fraud – bringing his total to 11 charges; on the same day the three linked companies were also charged with fraud (Aperia International five counts, Aperia Cloud Services (II) two, A-Speed Infotech one). Wei is accused of using more than $38 million of alleged ill-gotten gains to buy a landed property in Chee Hoon Avenue between July and October 2024, a good-class bungalow police earlier valued at around $55 million, and of acquiring more than $5 million in two bank accounts that year, about $3.2 million of which is alleged to be the benefits of purported criminal conduct. The item describes the case as allegedly linked to chipmaker Nvidia. Wei's lawyers said he and the companies will contest all charges; pre-trial conferences are set for 14 August.\n    Quote: SINGAPORE – A man accused of fraud in a case allegedly linked to chipmaker Nvidia is said to have used more than $38 million in purported ill-gotten gains to buy a landed property. … On July 6, Alan Wei Zhaolun, 50, was handed nine additional charges – three counts of money laundering and six counts of fraud. … The Singaporean former chief executive of a group of companies – A-Speed Infotech, Aper\n- e194 [fact; The Straits Times, citing a police spokesperson; original source Singapore Police Force (charge allegations) 2026-07-06; from gnews:f0d529209a91c7a237e117f1 2026-07-06] Singapore police said the fraud charges allege that Alan Wei Zhaolun conspired with Aaron Woon Guo Jie, 41, and Jenny Lim, 51, to commit fraud multiple times in 2023 and 2024, in a conspiracy linked to the purchase of servers from three suppliers – Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus – in which they falsely represented in communications to those suppliers that one of the Aperia Group companies would be the end-user of the servers. The charges are allegations; the defendants contest them.\n    Quote: According to his latest charges, he also allegedly conspired with Aaron Woon Guo Jie, 41, and Jenny Lim, 51, to commit fraud multiple times in 2023 and 2024. … The police said the fraud charges allege that the trio engaged in a conspiracy to commit fraud linked to the purchase of servers from three suppliers – Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus. … Without revealing further details, a police spoke\n- e202 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey of 60 Chinese executives) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released on Tuesday (Jul 7), found that respondents will allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today.\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months. … This is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7). … said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e245, e281, e441; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e204 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey of 60 Chinese executives) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] According to the Bloomberg Intelligence survey of Chinese executives, AI accelerators made by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were being evaluated by a large pool of respondents.\n    Quote: AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e284; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e205 [third_party_view; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg Intelligence (survey of 60 Chinese executives) 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey said that while NVIDIA's products remain popular, the company's market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over.\n    Quote: While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e282, e283; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e206 [fact; Bloomberg News; original source Chinese government (reported by Bloomberg News) ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country over the next five years under a government-led initiative to expand AI use, and at least 80% of core technologies such as chips will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management. … At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e286; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e236 [fact; e.vnexpress.net (citing Channel News Asia); original source Singapore Police Force 2026-07-01; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] The Singapore Police Force issued a prohibition of disposal order on July 1 (2026) freezing any sale or transfer of a S$55 million (US$42.5 million) Good Class Bungalow at 12 Chee Hoon Avenue, Singapore, allegedly bought with the proceeds of a scheme to smuggle servers believed to contain Nvidia AI chips that Washington bars from export to China; officers also seized about S$1 million from bank accounts tied to the case, per Channel News Asia.\n    Quote: Singapore police have seized a S$55 million (US$42.5 million) luxury bungalow allegedly bought with the proceeds of a scheme to smuggle servers believed to contain Nvidia artificial intelligence chips that Washington bars from export to China. … The Singapore Police Force issued a prohibition of disposal order on July 1 freezing any sale or transfer of the Good Class Bungalow, the city-state's mos\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e35; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e237 [fact; e.vnexpress.net (citing Bloomberg, Mothership, charge sheets); original source Singapore prosecutors (charge sheets) ; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] Prosecutors allege that Aperia Group chief executive Wei Zhaolun (Alan Wei) and two other Aperia executives deceived Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus between November 2023 and February 2025 by certifying on end-user forms that companies within the group would receive servers, in a case involving servers believed to contain Nvidia chips barred from export to China; Wei pleaded not guilty on July 6 and his lawyer called the charges \"misconceived.\"\n    Quote: Prosecutors say Wei and two other Aperia executives deceived Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus between November 2023 and February 2025 by certifying on end-user forms that companies within the group would receive the servers. … Aperia Group chief executive Wei Zhaolun, 50, known as Alan Wei, pleaded not guilty on July 6 to all 11 charges against him regarding the case and had his bail raised to \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e68; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e238 [party_statement; e.vnexpress.net (citing Malay Mail); original source K. Shanmugam, Singapore Minister for Home Affairs and Law ; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] Singapore Home Affairs and Law Minister K. Shanmugam, speaking when the first charges were laid in March 2025, said the servers at issue were routed through Singapore and shipped on to Malaysia; whether Malaysia was the final destination or a further transit point remains unconfirmed, and some reporting points to China, according to the item.\n    Quote: The servers were routed through Singapore and shipped on to Malaysia, Home Affairs and Law Minister K. Shanmugam said when the first charges were laid in March 2025, according to Malay Mail . … Whether Malaysia was the final destination or a further transit point remains unconfirmed, and some reporting points to China.\n- e239 [fact; e.vnexpress.net (citing The Diplomat); original source U.S. Commerce Department inquiry (as reported by The Diplomat) ; from gnews:c6445931955f107b551cce85 2026-07-07] The Singapore case grew out of US investigations, including a Commerce Department inquiry reported by The Diplomat, into whether Chinese AI firm DeepSeek obtained restricted Nvidia chips through intermediaries in Singapore and Malaysia; Singapore police have not confirmed any link between Aperia and DeepSeek.\n    Quote: The case grew out of U.S. investigations into whether Chinese AI firm DeepSeek obtained restricted Nvidia chips through intermediaries in Singapore and Malaysia, a Commerce Department inquiry reported by The Diplomat . … Singapore police have not confirmed any link between Aperia and DeepSeek.\n- e241 [rumour; Reuters; original source DeepSeek 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek is developing its own custom AI chip for inference rather than training, a project Reuters reported (citing three unnamed people) has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with the company quietly recruiting chip engineers and in talks with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers (no p