nd clients they may soon be able to obtain H200 chips, the sources said, indicating some progress in import reviews by Chinese authorities.\n- e554 [fact; Reuters (as reported by Tom's Hardware / Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government (export approval) 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported on 2026-07-14 that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-generation H200 'Hopper' AI chips. ZTE joins a group of roughly 10 Chinese companies with U.S. clearance for such purchases, which also includes Alibaba, Tencent, ByteDance and JD.com.\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \"Hopper\" chips. … ZTE joins a club that counts Alibaba, Tencent, ByteDance, and JD.com among the roughly 10-strong group of Chinese companies with U.S. clearance for those purchases.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e540; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e555 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Bruno Ferreira); original source U.S. government / Kingsoft Cloud subsidiary ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] According to the article, an apparent subsidiary of Kingsoft Cloud received U.S. approval to buy AMD AI accelerators equivalent to Nvidia's H200 (presumably Instinct MI300X-class chips).\n    Quote: Additionally, an apparent subsidiary of Kingsoft Cloud got approval to buy AMD accelerators equivalent to Nvidia's H200, presumably Instinct MI300X-class chips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e540; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e556 [third_party_view; Reuters (as reported by Tom's Hardware); original source Chinese authorities (stance), reported via Reuters ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported there was no word on whether Chinese authorities will authorize ZTE's H200 imports, noting China has taken a protectionist stance as it tries to grow its own chip industry, discouraging purchases of foreign technology and pushing firms toward homegrown accelerators such as Huawei's, which the article says has made strides technologically and financially.\n    Quote: Over on the Chinese side of the table, Reuters remarks that there's no word on whether the respective authorities will give ZTE the go-ahead for import, as the country has taken on a protectionist stance as it tries to grow its own chip industry. … The country has discouraged firms from purchasing foreign tech and has instead pushed companies to acquire homegrown accelerators. Huawei in particular\n- e557 [party_statement; CNBC (as reported by Tom's Hardware); original source U.S. trade official (unnamed) 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official, cited by CNBC and speaking at a congressional hearing on 2026-07-14, said that 'very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place' and that it is 'a very small quantity of chips.'\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It’s a very small quantity of chips\" during a congressional hearing.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e490; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e573 [rumour; Reuters (via Business Times), citing the Financial Times; original source Financial Times 2026-07-14; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new \"white list\" of companies that passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14), citing three unnamed people; Reuters could not immediately verify the report and Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment.\n    Quote: NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14). … Reuters could not immediately verify the report. Nvidia did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e522; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e574 [rumour; Reuters (via Business Times), citing the Financial Times; original source Financial Times 2026-07-14; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] Over the past few months NVIDIA has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, and after pressure from Washington has tightened compliance procedures, with staff now visiting customers' data centres, verifying contracts and interviewing end users as part of the checks, according to the Financial Times report citing three unnamed people familiar with the matter.\n    Quote: Over the past few months, the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, the report added, citing three people familiar with the matter. … Nvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\n- e575 [fact; Reuters (via Business Times); original source US Commerce Department ; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia's Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\n    Quote: The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 5. The evidence still implies spending above $1 trillion: the roughly $650 billion 2026 plan followed by NVIDIA's forecast points to growth of more than 53.8%\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is no change (rung 3): no consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Continued supply tightness through most of 2027 points to a further increase, but the exceptional 2026 price run-up leaves a much higher comparison base; I esti\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the currently reported position—no public Chinese authorization is reported—which sits at rung 2. The specific reports that China planned to permit limited purchases for named firms, followed by Reuters-reported progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s,\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: No CY2026 NVIDIA China-share consensus is supplied. The nearest market reference is the reported current status quo of zero share and zero China data-center compute revenue, which sits at rung 5. The forecast remains above that reference but below 5%: current zero shipments and domestic-supplier substitution weigh heavily, while reported plans for limited H200 purchases leave some possibility of sales in the remaining 2026. The reported ceiling of fewer than 200,000 chips is not a confirmed authorization or shipments in hand, and the evidence supplies no CY2026 spending denominator or chip price to translate it into a precise share.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "0a7b5c0dd2ee20520a6bc66da6a2c910111ea1e57de2caae2175617c092fac41": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v18 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra generation (Kyber NVL144 racks) begins shipping by the end of calendar 2027\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Rubin Ultra generation, in Kyber NVL144 racks, by December 31, 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027. / rung 2 No: NVIDIA has not begun shipping Rubin Ultra systems by December 31, 2027.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 18 items]\n[Not shown: 26 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e92 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, reported by CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the rack-scale system slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra generation in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. SemiAnalysis added that NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\n    Quote: Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap. … \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challengi\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e106, e128, e177, e182, e186, e192, e195, e212, e229, e323, e437, e559; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e93 [party_statement; NVIDIA, reported by CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e108, e132, e185, e200, e213, e230, e269, e324, e352, e400, e438, e560; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e94 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis, reported by CNBC; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together to reach similar compute power was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and operationally burdensome, leaving Nvidia, in the firm's words, with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.\"\n    Quote: A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. … \"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\" SemiAnalysis said. … That leaves Nvidia with \"no p\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107, e129, e178, e183, e188, e196; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e128 [third_party_view; datacenterdynamics.com (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028 from an originally planned 2027, citing manufacturability problems with the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules.\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. … However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the p\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e130 [third_party_view; datacenterdynamics.com (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that the manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, likely delaying it or limiting it to small volumes, and said Nvidia is left with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\n    Quote: The manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. … All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e187, e197; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e131 [third_party_view; datacenterdynamics.com (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis separately reported, about a week earlier, that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design due to 'manufacturing execution concerns,' and that the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e179, e184, e198; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e180 [third_party_view; the-decoder.com, citing SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia has no proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now.\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e200 [party_statement; Nvidia representative, via Tom's Hardware; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571 2026-07-06] An Nvidia representative responded to Tom's Hardware's questions about the reported Kyber delay with the statement \"Our roadmap is intact,\" providing no further details; the article noted it is unclear whether this refers to the original roadmap and delivery schedules or to a revised roadmap.\n    Quote: An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \"Our roadmap is intact.\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions. As such, it is unclear if Nvidia's statement refers to its original roadmap, planned delivery schedules, and hardware, or if those particulars had already been changed and the statement refers to a newly updated roadmap.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e229 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St. (Danielle Liverance); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 (a post CNBC cited) that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed from a planned 2027 launch to 2028. SemiAnalysis attributed the delay to manufacturing difficulty with the system's PCB midplane (orthogonal backplane), a printed circuit board of up to 78 layers, and also said the larger NVL576, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes.\n    Quote: Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028. CNBC cited the SemiAnalysis post. … A larger sibling, the NVL576, designed to link eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes, according to SemiAnalysis. … The reported culpri\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e270 [party_statement; NVIDIA spokesperson, reported by Futuriom; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] An NVIDIA spokesperson said, in response to the Kyber setback report, that \"Our road map is intact\" and that \"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\"\n    Quote: An NVIDIA spokesperson said, “Our road map is intact.” Further, \"Vera Rubin Ultra will scale up to a larger NVL576 domain in its planned 2027 timeline.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e335; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e143 (); both are in force: 24/7 Wall St. reported NVIDIA shares were under pressure on reports of delays across the upcoming lineup of its Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are attributed to unnamed sources.\n- e335 [party_statement; IEEE Spectrum (Knvul Sheikh); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:a4eb2bdfabca94f5887881c6 2026-07-09] Nvidia is planning to increase the number of processors in a single interconnected (scale-up) system from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027, per IEEE Spectrum's report, as bandwidth and GPU counts per AI system keep rising.\n    Quote: At the same time, Nvidia is planning to add even more processors to single interconnected systems, going from 72 GPUs today to as many as 576 by 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e270; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e399 [rumour; Jim Cramer, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Semi Analysis (and unnamed reports) ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] Reports indicated a delay to NVIDIA's next-generation AI chips. Per Jim Cramer, the research firm Semi Analysis said 'everything could be late for NVIDIA because one particular part of their business is late,' referring to the Kyber platform. The reports are unconfirmed and Cramer noted he could not prove the story.\n    Quote: AI giant NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is currently in the news these days due to reports of a delay to its next generation AI chips. … this Kyber delay, what's changed, what it means, what happened. You know Semi Analysis does pretty good work, saying that everything could be late for NVIDIA because one particular part of their business is late.\n- e400 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA Corp. ; from gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b 2026-07-11] NVIDIA denied reports that its Kyber platform is being delayed, a company statement that the platform's schedule still stands.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) has denied reports that the Kyber platform is being delayed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e437 [third_party_view; Geoffrey Seiler (fool.com); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Technology research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 next-generation AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 because of troubles manufacturing a crucial circuit board, per fool.com's account.\n    Quote: This has been a key part of CEO Jensen Huang's strategy; however, it could have hit a snag when technology research outfit SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e438 [party_statement; Geoffrey Seiler (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3cd991655d4cdeaa4a578b84 2026-07-12] Nvidia said its technology roadmap remains intact, in response to reports questioning the Kyber NVL144 schedule; the article notes questions linger.\n    Quote: Nvidia has come out and said its roadmap remains intact, although questions linger.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e446 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] Nvidia said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture; the item gives no shipping date.\n    Quote: The company said development of its next-generation Rubin Ultra platform remains on schedule and confirmed there have been no changes to its NVLink architecture.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e92 (); both are in force: Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the rack-scale system slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra generation in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. SemiAnalysis added that NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\n- e559 [rumour; EBC (ebc.com), citing reports from SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Over the weekend before the 2026-07-14 article, reports attributed to industry research firm SemiAnalysis circulated suggesting Nvidia's upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028; the report is unconfirmed by Nvidia.\n    Quote: Over the weekend, reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e560 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, via EBC (ebc.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] An Nvidia spokesperson denied the SemiAnalysis delay report, stating that the company's \"roadmap is intact\".\n    Quote: Although an Nvidia spokesperson quickly countered the reports, telling that the company's \"roadmap is intact,\" the seeds of doubt were already sown.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 5. The evidence still implies spending above $1 trillion: the roughly $650 billion 2026 plan followed by NVIDIA's forecast points to growth of more than 53.8%\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is no change (rung 3): no consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Continued supply tightness through most of 2027 points to a further increase, but the exceptional 2026 price run-up leaves a much higher comparison base; I esti\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the currently reported position—no public Chinese authorization is reported—which sits at rung 2. The specific reports that China planned to permit limited purchases for named firms, followed by Reuters-reported progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s,\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes); no separate market consensus is supplied. The specific SemiAnalysis report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because of PCB-midplane manufacturing difficulty, together with the reported cancellation of the backup rack design, implies no shipment by December 31, 2027. NVIDIA’s statements that its roadmap is intact and Rubin Ultra development is on schedule are meaningful counterweights, but they give no specific Kyber NVL144 shipping date; the NVL576 2027 statement does not establish NVL144 shipments. The FY2027 Data Center revenue conclusion is compatible with this forecast and does not require Kyber NVL144 to ship.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "f8e22979ba10a54ee1ee78ce1e151429e3e4a758df6d0cbe76fb79ce6cd415df": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v19 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 revenue from standalone Vera CPUs\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much revenue will NVIDIA report from standalone Vera CPUs in FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $12 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $6 billion and below $12 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is at least $2 billion and below $6 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue is below $2 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 15 items]\n- e4 [fact; TradingKey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01] NVIDIA selected Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, a product-architecture decision reported by TradingKey on July 1, 2026; the article describes the DGX Rubin NVL8 as the AI system intended to power data centres over the next two to three years and for the next product cycle, but gives no shipment timing or unit volumes.\n    Quote: NVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. … NVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next \n- e5 [party_statement; TradingKey; original source Lip-Bu Tan, CEO of Intel ; from gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01] Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan said recently that the CPU has become the orchestration layer and control plane of AI infrastructure and that, in inference workloads, the CPU-to-GPU ratio is shifting from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU, meaning the number of CPUs needed to support each GPU is rising; the item gives no date beyond 'recently' and cites no data behind the ratio.\n    Quote: CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role). … \n- e6 [fact; TradingKey; original source Google and Intel ; from gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a 2026-07-01] Google signed a multiyear deal, announced with its Q1 2026 results, to keep using Intel Xeon processors in its workload-optimized instances, including Xeon 6 in its C4 and N4 instances, and is co-developing custom ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips with Intel; Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan said the agreements span three to five years with locked volume and pricing. The article elsewhere calls the co-developed chips 'custom AI accelerators (ASIC)', while the deal description specifies IPUs.\n    Quote: Google inked a multiyear deal for continued use of Intel Xeon processors in Google's workload-optimized instances, including the newest Xeon 6 processors that power C4 and N4 instances. The companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks. … CEO Tan sai\n- e76 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] Nvidia announced in its first fiscal quarter of 2027 that it plans to become much more involved in the CPU market, and management said it thinks it can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales. The item does not state the period over which those sales would be earned.\n    Quote: Furthermore, in its first fiscal quarter of 2027, Nvidia announced plans to become much more involved in the central processing unit (CPU) space , which has been in high demand due to the rise of agentic AI. … Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e99 [party_statement; Fool.com (Adria Cimino); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] Per the article, Nvidia forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year (2026), reflecting its push into the data-center CPU market with its first stand-alone CPU. This is the company's own forecast and 'this year' is not further defined.\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e76; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e100 [party_statement; Fool.com (Adria Cimino); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] According to the article, Nvidia is launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall (2026) as part of its Vera Rubin platform.\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\n- e122 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research (the bank) ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\n    Quote: The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\n- e208 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source Perplexity 2026-07-07; from nvblog:nvidia-vera-max-single-threaded-cpu-at-scale 2026-07-07] In its corporate blog, NVIDIA said that AI search company Perplexity has adopted the NVIDIA Vera CPU: Perplexity tested Vera on the agentic coding work it runs daily and is now looking to deploy Vera in its upcoming production system. No deployment volume, timing, contract value or revenue is disclosed.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Vera exemplifies a new class of CPU, architected for the era of agents and being adopted by AI innovators including Perplexity; NVIDIA CPU roadmap continues with the NVIDIA Rosa CPU and its Rigel core. … Perplexity tested Vera on the agentic work it runs every day. … Perplexity is now looking to deploy Vera in its upcoming production system.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377, e397; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e298 [party_statement; Bruno Ferreira, Tom's Hardware; original source AMD ; from gnews:9fbd0f954b9cc2281da531d1 2026-07-08] AMD responded to Phoronix's Vera benchmarking by releasing its own metrics claiming a 3.3x performance gain over Nvidia's Vera CPU for the projected output of a 100 kW rack of its hardware.\n    Quote: But AMD had some things to say about the Phoronix test, firing back with its own metrics of a 3.3x performance gain over Vera for the projected output of a 100 kW rack of its hardware.\n- e363 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:6abaf2856736877236d30f6a 2026-07-10] NVIDIA management said its new Vera CPU opens a \"brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA,\" a market the company says it has never addressed before.\n    Quote: Management sees its new Vera CPU opening a “brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA,” a market it has never addressed before.\n- e377 [fact; quantumzeitgeist.com; original source Perplexity ; from gnews:b666d35e5247a8ddcc2d7c72 2026-07-10] AI company Perplexity is reported to be among the first customers to adopt NVIDIA's newly unveiled Vera CPU and is now planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems. Perplexity tested Vera on real coding workflows, where Vera completed the job (cloning a repository and running its test suite in sandboxes) about 1.5x faster than x86 and started concurrent sandboxes up to 1.9x faster.\n    Quote: AI innovator Perplexity is among the first to adopt it. … Perplexity tested Vera on real-world coding workflows, achieving a 1.5 times speed increase in cloning repositories and running test suites, and Perplexity started concurrent sandboxes up to 1.9 times faster. … Perplexity is now planning to integrate Vera into its upcoming production systems, demonstrating confidence in its ability to enhan\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e396 [party_statement; tikr.com, citing NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA said it expects its Vera CPU line to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.\n    Quote: Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e76; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e397 [fact; tikr.com; original source Perplexity ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] AI startup Perplexity has chosen to use NVIDIA's new CPUs for AI agent tasks.\n    Quote: AI startup Perplexity has also chosen to use Nvidia’s new CPUs for AI agent tasks.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e430 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source Wedbush Securities ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] Wedbush Securities argued recently that NVIDIA's new Vera CPU platform could significantly expand NVIDIA's total addressable market beyond graphics processors into traditional server CPUs, networking infrastructure and complete AI computing platforms, opening the door to billions of dollars in additional revenue. The item gives no specific TAM figure or timeframe.\n    Quote: Wedbush Securities recently argued that Nvidia's new Vera CPU platform could expand the company's total addressable market (TAM) well beyond graphics processors, opening the door to billions of dollars in additional revenue. … Wedbush believes this move could significantly increase Nvidia's total addressable market. Instead of competing only in AI accelerators, Nvidia can now target traditional se\n- e431 [rumour; Barchart.com; original source Barchart.com (unnamed-source report) ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] According to unnamed reports cited by the article, NVIDIA has begun offering its new Vera data-center processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow; the item gives no customers, volumes or terms.\n    Quote: Reports also suggest Nvidia has begun offering the new processors to cloud customers, including some in China where regulations allow.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish; reasons: The market reference is the FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $392.7 billion, translated using Q1's approximately 92% Data Center mix to about $361 billion; that reference sits in rung 2. The implied forecast is about $397–398 billion: the current total-revenue dependency estimate of $431–433 billion multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%. That is below the $440 billion rung-1 boundary and above $360 billion, and Data Center remains below the whole-company revenue estimate. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide support the\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-14; reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 5. The evidence still implies spending above $1 trillion: the roughly $650 billion 2026 plan followed by NVIDIA's forecast points to growth of more than 53.8%\n- v16 (custom-silicon, due 2027-03-31) How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: There is no direct market consensus or company guidance for aggregate calendar-2026 cloud-provider ASIC spending, so the reference is unplaced on this ladder. I forecast roughly $80–85 billion: Broadcom's $16 billion quarterly AI-revenue guide and rapid growth support a much larger supplier run-rate\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the reported plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which is in rung 3. The evidence still points to spending near that level: Google’s capex guidance and Meta’s raised capex budget support upward pressure, while strong cloud growth and the bullish Nvidia-revenue dependency ar\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 2 Up 10% to 30%; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is no change (rung 3): no consensus or company guidance for 2027-versus-2026 server DRAM and HBM contract prices is supplied. Continued supply tightness through most of 2027 points to a further increase, but the exceptional 2026 price run-up leaves a much higher comparison base; I esti\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the currently reported position—no public Chinese authorization is reported—which sits at rung 2. The specific reports that China planned to permit limited purchases for named firms, followed by Reuters-reported progress in import reviews and firms saying they may soon obtain H200s,\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU sales forecast by the end of FY2027, which sits at rung 1's $20 billion boundary. The carried estimate of 1.6–2 million CPUs at Morgan Stanley's approximately $5,000 per-unit price implies about $8–10 billion, in rung 3. The target would require about 4 million CPUs at that price; the fall launch and prospective, unsized customer deployments do not establish that volume. Intel's host-CPU selection and AMD's claimed performance advantage are competitive risks, while the broad Data Center estimate in v2 is a whole-segment figure and does not imply a comparable standalone Vera share. The relevant AI-capex themes support demand but do not change the implied standalone CPU volume enough to cross a rung boundary.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."}], "response_format": {"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name": "judgement", "strict": true, "schema": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["rung", "reference", "implied_outcome", "reasons", "cites", "cites_views", "pushes", "changed", "why_changed", "theme_pushes"], "properties": {"rung": {"type": "integer"}, "reasons": {"type": "string"}, "cites": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "cites_views": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["evidence_id", "push"], "properties": {"evidence_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "changed": {"type": "boolean"}, "why_changed": {"type": "string"}, "theme_pushes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "required": ["view_id", "push"], "properties": {"view_id": {"type": "string"}, "push": {"type": "string", "enum": ["toward_first", "toward_last", "none"]}}}}, "reference": {"type": "string"}, "implied_outcome": {"type": "string"}}}}}, "max_completion_tokens": 16000, "reasoning_effort": "medium"}, "10d533c99c9deffe3421dff2e1f202b196793a46d26e013f2a8c85ac75279977": {"model": "gpt-6-luna", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thesis v17 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending in calendar 2026\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What share of China's calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 30%. / rung 2 Bullish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 15% and below 30%. / rung 3 Neutral: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 5% and below 15%. / rung 4 Bearish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is at least 1% and below 5%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: NVIDIA's share of China's data-center AI accelerator spending is below 1%.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 64 items]\n[Not shown: 1 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e36 [fact; BBC; original source US government ; from gnews:a6ed98dfdf1ad76f6edffdfd 2026-07-02] The BBC reports that the US has since approved the sale of some of Nvidia's semiconductors to China, under certain conditions.\n    Quote: The US has since approved the sale of some of Nvidia's semiconductors to China, under certain conditions .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46, e491; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e46 [fact; The Wire China; original source Trump administration ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December, after the U.S. government had previously restricted such sales under Biden-era export controls on the chip (launched in 2023).\n    Quote: President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e47 [party_statement; The Wire China; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA) ; from gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02 2026-07-02] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has said that, since the Biden administration first imposed AI-chip export controls in 2022, Nvidia's market share in China has fallen to zero.\n  